// TypeResolution — the Maxon-tier `resolveTypes` pass. Replaces every
// unresolved / named `MaxonType` with a concrete one so that `lowerMaxonToStd`
// (whose `maxonTypeToStdType` panics on `named`/`unresolved`) never sees an
// unresolved type, and keeps the `funcReturnTypes` registry in sync with the
// resolved module so SemanticCheck reads resolved types.
//
// ⭐ **THE CASCADE BELOW IS `denotedNamedType`, AND IT HAS THREE READERS, NOT ONE.** "What does this
// name denote?" is asked by this pass (whose `unknown` verdict IS its E3011) AND by the two places a
// program writes an `as`-cast target — `Parser.applyCast` for a body cast and `Parser.applyConstCast`
// for a top-level `let`'s. Those two used to ask nothing at all: `parseTypeReference` hands back a
// bare `MaxonType.named` for every type it cannot answer syntactically, `maxonTypeTag` reads all of
// them as one tag, and so `5 as CompletelyMadeUpNameXyz` compiled and returned 5. The cure is the ONE
// list, asked from all three — never a second cascade in the parser, which is this project's
// signature bug (one fact written down twice) aimed at the question where the two copies would look
// most alike.
//
// It reads the WHOLE-PROGRAM signature index rather than the `Project` registries, and that is what
// makes it askable from the parser at all (the parser holds no `Project` — the pure-parser
// invariant). The two hold the same declarations, folded from the same artifacts through the same
// doors: `upsertDeclaredEnum` is the single write of BOTH enum registries and `createEnumRegistry`
// seeds both, and the parser has ALREADY been deciding struct-ness from the index for every `named`
// parameter and return type (`Parser.resolveNamedStruct`), so reading it here makes resolution agree
// with the parser rather than merely with itself.
//
// A named type resolves through FOUR sources, in order:
//
//   1. The BUILTIN alias `ExitCode`, recognized by NAME — WITHOUT loading the stdlib
//      (full stdlib type resolution is deferred until programs call stdlib functions).
//      It resolves to `MaxonType.exitCode`, a width-free tag whose unsigned-32-bit
//      width is assigned at the Maxon→Std boundary (see `maxonTypeToStdType`).
//   2. The project's DECLARED STRUCT registry — every `type Point` the parser declared. It
//      resolves to `MaxonType.structRef(id)`, KEEPING the name id, so the identity survives
//      resolution rather than having to be recovered from a column. `Point` and `Rect` are
//      different types however identical their fields, and the id is what says so.
//   3. The project's DECLARED ENUM/UNION registry — every `enum`/`union` the parser declared. It
//      resolves to `MaxonType.integer` (an enum value is its i64 tag), DROPPING the id like an
//      alias does: nothing downstream needs to know a value was a `Color`, because only `match`
//      inspects an enum and it reads the enum by name from the signature index at parse time.
//   4. The project's RANGED TYPEALIAS registry — every `typealias Score = int(0 to 100)`
//      the parser declared. An alias resolves to its UNDERLYING primitive: resolution
//      erases the NAME and the RANGE alike, because neither survives into a machine type.
//      Both are still facts about the program — the name is the TYPE (`Age` never reaches
//      a `Year` slot without an `as`) and the range is what a narrowing conversion is
//      guarded against — and both are read BEFORE this pass, off the value columns and the
//      pre-erasure signature columns.
//
// A named type in none of the four is a USER error (a typo, or a type this slice does not
// have), not a compiler-invariant violation, and it is E3011. WHERE it is anchored is the
// asking reader's to decide, which is why `denotedNamedType` raises `UnknownTypeName` rather
// than reporting: a DECLARATION site reports unpositioned (nothing records a source span for a
// type reference in a signature or a field — see `resolveNamedType`), while a CAST site is
// anchored on the `as` the user wrote, because the parser holds that token when it asks.
//
// The integer literal `42` is already typed `integer` (→ i64) by the parser, so it
// needs no resolution.

// The one builtin alias resolved without the stdlib. Named constant, not a bare
// string — the alias spelling is a contract shared with the parser's interner. Grows
// into a builtin-alias table (Character, etc.) when the stdlib cone lands.
//
// ⚠ **FILE-PRIVATE, AND IT IS THE `builtinTypeNameTag` BELOW THAT CARRIES THE NAME ACROSS FILES (W1).** The
// re-derivation doors — an interface's rendered return STRING, a function typealias's stored return NAME —
// need the ANSWER, not the bytes, and handing them the bytes is what produced three hand-written copies of
// one rule and a wasm trap in two of them. Nothing outside this file names the constant now; the doors ask
// the table, which is the only thing that keeps them from drifting (P1.7a slice 2b-iv-B, W1).
let ExitCodeBuiltinName = b"ExitCode"

// ⭐⭐ **THE TAG A BUILTIN TYPE NAME DENOTES — THE ONE ANSWER FOR EVERY DOOR THAT HOLDS A TYPE ONLY AS ITS
// SOURCE-SPELLED NAME (W1).** Two registries store a declared type INTERNER-FREE, as the bytes the source
// wrote: an INTERFACE method's return type (a rendered source STRING — `IrInterface`) and a FUNCTION
// TYPEALIAS's parameters and return (`FunctionTypeAlias`, whose `(tag, name)` pairs are what let it ride the
// merge with no interner remap). Both therefore RE-DERIVE what a declaration derived once, and a
// re-derivation is sound only while it reaches the SAME answer.
//
// It reaches the same answer for every name but one, because a ranged alias, an enum, a struct and a bare
// nominal `named` all collapse to the same i64 machine word whichever route arrives at them
// (`valueTagToStdType`).
//
// ⚠ **`ExitCode` IS THE ONE THAT DOES NOT, AND WHAT IT COST WAS A wasm TRAP.** It is the only builtin whose
// tag carries a SUB-64 width (u32 — `valueTagToStdType`'s `exitCode` arm is the sole home of that width), so
// a `returns ExitCode` rebuilt as a `named` is an i64 while the callee's own resolved declaration is a u32.
// Invisible on every register target, where both occupy one 64-bit GPR, and a hard
// **`wasm trap: indirect call type mismatch`** on wasm, whose `call_indirect` checks the declared functype
// against the funcref's own EXACTLY: the call site declared `() -> i64` against a `__fnref_` thunk typed
// `() -> i32`. MEASURED both ways — `typealias Thunk = function() returns ExitCode` + `nine() returns
// ExitCode` returned 9 natively and trapped 134 under wasmtime, while the identical program over a user
// `int(0 to u32.max)` alias of the SAME RANGE ran clean on both. That pair is what says the fact is a WIDTH
// recovered from a NAME, not a name treated specially, and both halves of it are pinned
// (`specs-shv2/first-class-functions.md`, `exitcode-return-through-alias` + its `ranged-alias` control).
//
// ⇒ **The rule is stated ONCE, here, and asked by all three doors** — `Parser.interfaceReturnMaxonType`,
// `Parser.functionAliasReturnType`, and this file's own `functionAliasReturnType`/`functionAliasParamType`
// twin (which rebuild against the project's merged interner where the parser's rebuilds against the FILE's).
// It had been spelled at the first of the three and nowhere else, which is exactly why the other two dropped
// the width; a fourth hand-written `ExitCode` arm would have passed the same test and left standing the
// arrangement that produced the trap.
//
// ⚠ **`named` IS THE ANSWER FOR EVERY OTHER NAME, AND IT IS A CLASSIFICATION RATHER THAN A SENTINEL**: it
// says the name denotes a NAMED type, which is precisely what `maxonTypeOfTag`'s own `named` arm rebuilds
// from the caller's interned id. Every caller feeds this straight into that rebuild, so there is no second
// decision to keep in step and nothing to test the answer against.
//
// ⚠⚠ **BUT THE FALL-THROUGH IS SILENT ON EVERY REGISTER TARGET, AND ONLY THE wasm LANE MAKES IT LOUD (W1
// review).** A name that BELONGS here and is missing gets `named` — an i64 — and an i64 and a u32 occupy the
// same GPR, so x64 and arm64 compute the right answer and say nothing; wasm's `call_indirect` is the only
// reader that checks. That is not a reason to distrust the classification, it is the reason the wasm lane is
// a gate: the omission this table exists to prevent was found by wasm and by nothing else. `denotedNamedType`
// now sources its own builtin arm from here, so a name the compiler resolves at all cannot be added there and
// forgotten here — which closes the direction the omission was actually made in.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE ANSWER IS ONLY VALID AT A DOOR THAT HOLDS A *RENDERED* TYPE NAME — NOT AT ONE ASKING WHAT A
// `named` REFERENCE DENOTES.** `bool`/`int`/`float`/`String` are unreachable as a `named` reference's name
// (the first three are KEYWORDS; `String` and `Character` are settled syntactically by
// `Parser.parseTypeReference`), so they can only arrive here from a rendered interface signature — where
// they mean the builtin. That is why `denotedNamedType` admits only the `exitCode` answer and not every
// non-`named` one; a new caller must decide the same question before it may read past the `exitCode` arm.
//
// ⛔ **THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO REST THAT ON "a user `enum String` is legal (measured: it compiles and runs),
// and its `Self` arrives as `named("String")`", AND BOTH HALVES ARE NOW FALSE (W17-b).** RE-MEASURED:
// `enum String` is **`E2015 … a declaration of the type name 'String', which the compiler owns`** —
// `Parser.requireTypeNameNotCompilerOwned` refuses it, because `String` and `Character` joined
// `isCompilerOwnedTypeName` (BATCH2 slice 2) after that sentence was written. And a `Self` inside the one
// body that MAY wear the name — the corpus's own `type String` — is now the builtin tag rather than any
// `named` reference (`Parser.enclosingSelfType`, which asks `byteRecordTagOfTypeName`). So the restriction
// above stands on the STRUCTURAL fact instead, which is stronger than the counter-example was: neither name
// can wear a `named` tag at all, in any program, so the answers past `exitCode` are unreachable here rather
// than merely risky. A negative claim that ages is exactly what this file's own header warns a
// re-derivation costs.
//
// ⚠ **`Character` IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, and admitting it would be a FEATURE rather than a re-spelling.**
// The set here is exactly the set the interface door already answered for, because this rung single-sources
// that door and does not widen it. A function typealias cannot reach the question at all:
// `parseTypeReference` answers `Character` (and `String`) SYNTACTICALLY, so an alias stores them by TAG with
// an EMPTY name and the `named` arm is never asked about either. ⭐ That is not an argument but a
// MEASUREMENT (W1 review): `typealias CharThunk = function() returns Character` and its `String` twin both
// compile and run correctly on the host AND on wasm, which is only possible by the TAG route — the name
// column is empty, so this table is never consulted. Both are pinned
// (`first-class-function.character-return-through-alias`, `.string-return-through-alias`), so a future change
// that started storing them by NAME would turn this reasoning red instead of quietly relying on it. The
// interface door can reach it, and
// MEASURED it is REFUSED — an interface method declared `returns Character`, dispatched through a witness,
// reports `E3005 Cannot return 'int' from function declared to return 'Character'`. That is a clean refusal
// rather than a wrong answer, and admitting it means adopting a managed record as an owned temp at the
// dispatch site (`Parser.enrolOwnedCallTemp`, which the witness door reaches through
// `mintWitnessDispatchResult`), which is a mechanism with its own acceptance cases.
//
// ⚠ Every name is spelled by the CANONICAL constant the compiler already exports for it, never a fresh
// `b"…"` — `BoolConformerName`'s own header says a second spelling of one of these "is the same fact and so
// must not become a second constant".
//
// ⛔⛔ **AND THE THREE PRIMITIVE ROWS ARE NOW *DELEGATED*, NOT RESTATED (W133 review), BECAUSE THE PARAGRAPH
// ABOVE STOPPED BEING TRUE OF THIS FUNCTION THE DAY A SECOND name→tag TABLE EXISTED.** Spelling each name
// through its canonical CONSTANT stops two rows disagreeing about the BYTES `int` is written in; it does
// nothing about two TABLES disagreeing about which names are in the set at all. W133 added
// `IrInterface.primitiveTagOfTypeName`, whose `int`/`float`/`bool` rows answered
// `integer`/`float`/`boolean` — the identical three rows this function already carried, in another file,
// with nothing forcing them to agree. Two hand-written `equals` chains over one set is this project's
// signature bug, and the failure mode is not a compile error: a fourth primitive added to one table is a
// value the OTHER door silently classifies `named`, so the extension door would admit a target whose written
// type reference resolves to a struct reference no registry holds — which is the very defect W133's
// `enclosingSelfType` arm exists to close, reintroduced one door over.
//
// ⇒ The set is stated ONCE, in `primitiveTagOfTypeName`, and this function adds only the two rows that are
// genuinely its own (`String`, `ExitCode` — see the two ⚠ paragraphs above for why `Character` is not a
// third). The delegation runs in this direction because it is the only one that exists: this file already
// reads `IrInterface`'s constants, so it may read its functions, while `IrInterface` calling back here would
// be a cycle.
//
// The MISS SENTINELS deliberately differ and must not be merged: a tag table's no-answer is `unresolved`,
// while a door holding a RENDERED TYPE NAME has already established that the name denotes *some* type, so
// its no-answer is `named` — the positive statement "this is a declared type", which is what every caller
// here branches on.
export function builtinTypeNameTag(name ByteArray) returns ValueTypeTag
	let primitive = primitiveTagOfTypeName(name)
	if primitive != ValueTypeTag.unresolved 'aPrimitive'
		return primitive
	end 'aPrimitive'

	if name.equals(StringConformerName) 'isString'
		return ValueTypeTag.string
	end 'isString'
	if name.equals(ExitCodeBuiltinName) 'isExitCode'
		return ValueTypeTag.exitCode
	end 'isExitCode'

	return ValueTypeTag.named
end 'builtinTypeNameTag'

// ⭐ **THE ONE REBUILD OF A TYPE STORED AS AN INTERNER-FREE `(tag, NAME)` PAIR** — the shape
// `FunctionTypeAlias` keeps for its parameters and its return (see there for why it travels by name rather
// than by id). The stored TAG already answers for every type whose spelling `parseTypeReference` settled
// syntactically; a `named` tag is the only one that still carries a NAME, so it is the only one asked
// whether that name is a builtin whose own tag carries a width. `typeNames` is the caller's OWN interner —
// the parser's file-local one during a parse, the project's merged one at resolution — and supplying it is
// the whole of what a caller does, which is what keeps the two tiers' rebuilds one function rather than two.
// It interns rather than taking an id because the empty-name rule below belongs to the storage convention.
//
// ⭐⭐ **WHICH IDENTITY A STORED ALIAS SLOT CARRIES BESIDE ITS NAME — the ONE classification, and it is a
// UNION so that adding a fourth kind STOPS THE BUILD at every reader (F3g review, D2).**
//
// Two readers dispatch on this and they live in different files: the REBUILD (`maxonTypeOfStoredTypeName`,
// which turns the slot back into a `MaxonType`) and the HASH (`ProgramSignatures.mixAliasReturnSlot`, which
// folds it into the parse-memo key). They cannot share a body — the reviewer measured why: routing the hash
// through the rebuild would make hashing INTERN into the index's own interner as a side effect, and
// `mixType`'s `named` arm skips `builtinTypeNameTag`, so `ExitCode` would hash differently by path.
//
// ⛔⛔ **SO THE DUPLICATION IS JUSTIFIED AND THE ENFORCEMENT WAS WHAT WAS MISSING.** Both were three-armed
// tag tests ending in a bare tag+name fallthrough, in two files, with nothing but a header promising they
// agreed. Add a fourth identity column to the rebuild alone and the hash falls silently through to
// `(tag, name)` for it: two different return types, one hash, no compile error anywhere — which is F3g's own
// defect reproduced against the next column. A union match must be EXHAUSTIVE, so a fourth case makes BOTH
// matches stop compiling and the reader has to answer for it in both places.
export union AliasSlotIdentity
	// The W14 DIGEST of `(declaring type, parameter name)` — not an interner id at all, which is why it needs
	// a column of its own rather than the name slot.
	typeParamToken(token TypeParamId)
	// The whole-program `GenericInstanceId` keying `GenericInstanceRegistry`. Validated by the classifier, so
	// no reader has to know that `NoGenericInstance` exists.
	genericInstance(id GenericInstanceId)
	// The tag and the name are the whole of the slot — every primitive, every `named`/`structRef`/`function`/
	// `interfaceRef`, and `void`.
	nameOnly
end 'AliasSlotIdentity'

// ⭐ **THE CLASSIFIER, AND THE ONE PLACE A MISSING IDENTITY IS REFUSED.** The `genericInstance` check lives
// here rather than at either reader because this is where the slot's CLAIM to be an instance is read: a slot
// that claims it and carries no id would otherwise rebuild as instance 0 at one reader and mix a raw
// `u64.max` at the other, which is two wrong answers from one bad record. See `NoGenericInstance`.
export function aliasSlotIdentity(tag ValueTypeTag, typeParamToken TypeParamId, genericInstanceId GenericInstanceId, slotName ByteArray) returns AliasSlotIdentity
	if tag == ValueTypeTag.typeParameter 'anOpaqueParameterOfTheDeclaringType'
		return AliasSlotIdentity.typeParamToken(typeParamToken)
	end 'anOpaqueParameterOfTheDeclaringType'

	if tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'anInstantiationOfAGenericBase'
		if genericInstanceId == NoGenericInstance 'theIdentityWasNeverCarried'
			panic("aliasSlotIdentity: a function alias slot tagged `genericInstance` (declared as '{String.from(slotName)}') carries no GenericInstanceId — the identity is minted beside the tag by `functionAliasInstanceId`, so a record reaching here without one was built by a route that did not fill the column, and reading it would silently name instance 0")
		end 'theIdentityWasNeverCarried'

		return AliasSlotIdentity.genericInstance(genericInstanceId)
	end 'anInstantiationOfAGenericBase'

	return AliasSlotIdentity.nameOnly
end 'aliasSlotIdentity'

// ⭐ **TWO SLOTS ARE THE ONES THE INTERNER CANNOT ANSWER FOR, AND NEITHER IS ANSWERED BY A CALLER
// (F3, F3g).** A `typeParameter`'s id column is the W14 digest; a `genericInstance`'s is its whole-program
// `GenericInstanceId`. Neither is an interner id, so each is carried beside the name in its own column
// (`FunctionAliasParam.typeParamToken`, `.genericInstanceId`) and substituted for the name's id here. Doing
// it in this one place is what keeps the property the header above claims: the two interners' rebuilds stay
// one function, and neither caller has to know that two arms of the union are not about interning.
//
// ⛔ The `genericInstance` arm's absence was a WRONG ANSWER rather than a refusal: without it a
// `genericInstance` fell through to `maxonTypeOfTag` carrying the NAME column's "no name" id —
// `UnnamedTypeId`, which is 0, which is a live instance. See `FunctionAliasParam.genericInstanceId`.
//
// ⚠ **FILE-PRIVATE SINCE F1 CONSOLIDATED THE TWO TIERS, AND THAT IS A CONSEQUENCE OF THE CONSOLIDATION
// RATHER THAN AN ENCAPSULATION CHOICE.** It was `export` while `Parser` had its own rebuild body calling it;
// now the only callers are the two slot doors below, so an `export` here would be `E3092: never referenced
// outside its declaring file` — which only a self-compile can see, because `checkUnusedExports` runs when
// shv2 compiles a PROGRAM and the bootstrap builds shv2 without it. The narrowing is also right on its
// merits: a caller holding a stored alias should reach the RETURN or PARAM door, which know that record's
// column list, rather than spell the columns itself.
function maxonTypeOfStoredTypeName(tag ValueTypeTag, name ByteArray, typeNames TypeNameInterner, typeParamToken TypeParamId, genericInstanceId GenericInstanceId) returns MaxonType
	match aliasSlotIdentity(tag, typeParamToken: typeParamToken, genericInstanceId: genericInstanceId, slotName: name) 'identity'
		typeParamToken(token) then return MaxonType.typeParameter(token)
		genericInstance(id) then return MaxonType.genericInstance(id)
		nameOnly then break 'identity'
	end 'identity'

	// ⭐ **THE EMPTY-NAME RULE, AND IT LIVES HERE BECAUSE IT IS PART OF THE STORAGE CONVENTION RATHER THAN
	// PART OF ANY CALLER (F1 review).** A primitive carries no name (`Parser.functionAliasTypeName`), and
	// `UnnamedTypeId` is what the name column means by that. It was spelled twice — inlined at the parser
	// tier, as a `functionAliasNameId` helper at the resolution tier — which is one rule with two homes and
	// the exact shape the two tiers' rebuild bodies were already in.
	let nameId = UnnamedTypeId if name.isEmpty() else typeNames.intern(name)
	return maxonTypeOfTag(tag if tag != ValueTypeTag.named else builtinTypeNameTag(name), nameId: nameId)
end 'maxonTypeOfStoredTypeName'

// ⭐⭐ **THE WHOLE STORED RETURN SLOT OF A FUNCTION ALIAS, REBUILT — THE ONE HOME OF THE VOID TEST AND OF THE
// COLUMN LIST (F1 review).** Both tiers rebuild the same stored alias and differ ONLY in which interner the
// names go into: the parser's file-local one during a parse, the project's merged one at resolution. That
// difference is the `typeNames` parameter and it is the whole of the difference.
//
// ⛔⛔ **IT WAS TWO BODIES, AND THE PROOF THAT THEY MUST MOVE IN LOCKSTEP IS THAT ADDING
// `returnGenericInstanceId` HAD TO EDIT BOTH.** Nothing but their headers made that happen. A sixth column
// added to one and not the other would have the two tiers rebuild ONE stored alias into two different
// `MaxonType`s — a wrong answer with no compile error anywhere, which is precisely the defect the
// `genericInstance` column above exists to have closed.
export function maxonTypeOfStoredAliasReturn(alias FunctionTypeAlias, typeNames TypeNameInterner) returns MaxonReturnType
	if alias.returnTag == ValueTypeTag.void 'returnsNothing'
		return MaxonReturnType.void
	end 'returnsNothing'

	return MaxonReturnType.value(maxonTypeOfStoredTypeName(alias.returnTag, name: alias.returnTypeName, typeNames: typeNames, typeParamToken: alias.returnTypeParamToken, genericInstanceId: alias.returnGenericInstanceId))
end 'maxonTypeOfStoredAliasReturn'

// ⭐ **THE PARAMETER TWIN OF THE RETURN DOOR ABOVE, AND THE PAIR IS THE POINT.** By CLAUDE.md's letter this
// is a thin wrapper — one delegation, one caller — and an inline call would name the same columns in the
// same one place, so "it holds the column mapping" is not what earns it. What earns it is that **the param
// and return halves of this record drifting apart is precisely this change's own defect class**: the return
// half is what decayed to instance 0, the param half decayed identically and had to be pinned by its own
// spec case because a fix to one would not have reached the other. A symmetric pair of doors is worth one
// line for a record whose two halves have already been shown to drift.
//
// ⚠ **FILE-PRIVATE where its twin is `export`, and the asymmetry is correct rather than an oversight.**
// `export` means "expected to be used outside this file" — the return door has real cross-file readers
// (`Parser.functionValueReturn`), this one is reached only from `resolveFunctionAliasShapes` below, and an
// `export` with no outside reader is `E3092`, which only the self-compile can see.
function maxonTypeOfStoredAliasParam(param FunctionAliasParam, typeNames TypeNameInterner) returns MaxonType
	return maxonTypeOfStoredTypeName(param.tag, name: param.typeName, typeNames: typeNames, typeParamToken: param.typeParamToken, genericInstanceId: param.genericInstanceId)
end 'maxonTypeOfStoredAliasParam'

// ⭐ **THE W14 TOKEN ONE SLOT OF A FUNCTION ALIAS'S SIGNATURE STORES — the mint that pairs with the rebuild
// above (F3).** `NoTypeParamId` for every tag but `typeParameter`, whose payload IS the token
// `Parser.parseTypeReference` already minted against the enclosing type's `uses` list, so this reads an
// identity rather than deriving a second one.
//
// ⚠ It takes the whole `MaxonType` rather than a tag plus an id because the pairing of the two is exactly
// what must not be re-spelled: reading the id column of a NON-type-parameter arm would file that arm's
// interner id as a digest, which is the shape W14 exists to have removed.
export function functionAliasTypeParamToken(declared MaxonType) returns TypeParamId
	if maxonTypeTag(declared) != ValueTypeTag.typeParameter 'notAnOpaqueParameter'
		return NoTypeParamId
	end 'notAnOpaqueParameter'

	return typeParamIdOf(declared)
end 'functionAliasTypeParamToken'

// ⭐ **THE `GenericInstanceId` ONE SLOT OF A FUNCTION ALIAS'S SIGNATURE STORES — `functionAliasTypeParamToken`'s
// twin, one tag over (F3g).** `NoGenericInstance` for every tag but `genericInstance`, whose payload IS the id
// `Parser.parseTypeReference` already interned against the whole-program registry, so this reads an identity
// rather than deriving a second one.
//
// ⚠ It takes the whole `MaxonType` for its twin's reason: reading the id column of a non-instance arm would
// file that arm's interner id as a gid, which is the shape this column exists to have removed.
export function functionAliasInstanceId(declared MaxonType) returns GenericInstanceId
	if maxonTypeTag(declared) != ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'notAnInstantiation'
		return NoGenericInstance
	end 'notAnInstantiation'

	return genericInstanceIdOf(declared)
end 'functionAliasInstanceId'

// `denotedNamedType`'s "this name is in none of the sources" answer. Payload-free — the caller
// already holds the name it asked about — so it is a scalar and the throw allocates nothing on a
// path taken once per named type reference in the program.
export enum UnknownTypeName implements Error
	notDeclared
end 'UnknownTypeName'

// Resolve every unresolved/named type in `module`'s function signatures to a
// concrete `MaxonType`, and re-sync each function's resolved return type into
// the project's `funcReturnTypes` registry (SemanticCheck's source of truth).
module function resolveTypes(project Project, module MaxonModule)
	// Fetched ONCE for the whole pass, not per named type: `denotedNamedType` reads it for every
	// reference, and the memoized query is a map probe that has no business being on that path. (It
	// used to be fetched inside `resolveNamedType`, on the rare qualified-alias arm only — this is
	// strictly fewer fetches, not more.)
	let signatures = try queryProgramSignatures(project) otherwise panic("resolveTypes: queryProgramSignatures failed — it is computed and memoized by compileToCodeResult before the pipeline runs, so it cannot fail fresh here")

	// FIRST in the first pass — before a single name is erased. Two of them name a TYPEALIAS, and
	// `resolveTypes` is what turns `Score` into `int`, so a drain that ran after it would have nothing
	// left to name. (v1 drains its equivalents inside its own TypeResolution for the same reason.)
	reportDeferredNameDiagnostics(project)

	// Index-based so each function is a mutable binding (a `for x in ...` loop
	// var is immutable; field assignment through it is rejected).
	for i in 0 upto module.functions.count() 'eachFunc'
		var func = try module.functions.get(i) otherwise panic("resolveTypes: functions.get OOB at {i}")
		func.maxonReturnType = resolveReturnType(project, signatures: signatures, ret: func.maxonReturnType, readerFilePath: func.sourceFilePath)

		var resolvedParams = MaxonTypeArray.create()
		for pt in func.maxonParamTypes 'eachParam'
			resolvedParams.push(resolveType(project, signatures: signatures, t: pt, readerFilePath: func.sourceFilePath))
		end 'eachParam'
		func.maxonParamTypes = resolvedParams

		// ⚠ **NOT one of the derived per-callee maps A2l routed through `FunctionNameIndex`, and the
		// difference is which fact it is.** This RE-SYNCS a registry whose KEY SET was already fixed at
		// merge: `ParseStaging.commitFuncReturnTypes` filled it, `commitFuncSignatures` beside it is the
		// whole-program duplicate detection for both (E3006 at the later declaration), and this only
		// overwrites keys that are already there — with the RESOLVED type, which is the point. So there is
		// no tie here for an index to break, and the uniqueness rule it does rest on is owned one file over
		// rather than restated here.
		project.funcReturnTypes.upsert(func.name, value: func.maxonReturnType)
		syncResolvedSignature(project, func: func)
	end 'eachFunc'

	checkStructFieldTypes(project, signatures: signatures)
	resolveFunctionAliasShapes(project, signatures: signatures)

	// M1 op value-types are all concrete (`integer` literals) — nothing to stamp.
	// Op-type resolution (typed constants whose type is a named alias) grows at M9.
end 'resolveTypes'

// ⭐⭐ **THE DEFERRED NAME DIAGNOSTICS (P1.9) — UNUSED TYPEALIASES FIRST, HIDDEN CAST TARGETS SECOND.**
//
// The two orders this fixes are both load-bearing and neither can be had where the diagnostics are
// FOUND. `DeferredNameDiagnostic` carries the argument in full; the short of it is that the parser
// reports per file, the file walk is alphabetical, and the corpus pins a program whose E3062 is in
// `types.maxon` and whose E2003 is in `main.maxon` — so per-file reporting orders them by two
// FILENAMES. Draining here orders them by what they ARE.
//
// ⚠ It runs in a pass, and the pipeline's gate fires only BETWEEN passes, so both lists are drained
// before anything stops the build — which is the second half of the same requirement: two diagnostics
// in one program, in one order.
function reportDeferredNameDiagnostics(project Project)
	// ⚠ Captured BEFORE the loop, because the loop APPENDS to the very array `declarationAlreadyReported`
	// scans: without the bound, each E3062 emitted here would be a row every later candidate compares
	// against — turning k unused aliases into O(k²) comparisons, and (worse) letting one unused alias
	// suppress another that happens to share its line in a different file.
	let alreadyReported = project.diagnostics.count()

	// An INDEX loop, not `for … in`: the element is a BORROW of `project`, and the call below is
	// recorded as writing that same storage. ⚖ Ruling of 2026-08-26 — canonical note at
	// `IR/Std/ElimTrivialBlockArgs.elimTrivialBlockArgs`.
	for unusedIndex in 0 upto project.unusedTypeAliasDiagnostics.count() 'eachUnused'
		let entry = try project.unusedTypeAliasDiagnostics.get(unusedIndex) otherwise panic("reportDeferredNameDiagnostics: unusedIndex {unusedIndex} is below project.unusedTypeAliasDiagnostics.count()")
		// ⭐ **A DECLARATION THAT WAS ALREADY REFUSED IS NOT ALSO REPORTED AS UNUSED.** Of course it is
		// unused — nothing accepted it — so the second diagnostic adds no fact and points the reader at
		// the symptom instead of the cause. TWO tests, because there are two ways a declaration earns a
		// diagnostic and they are not the same shape:
		//
		//   • **By NAME**, for a name declared TWICE (E3061/E3006): the collision explains EVERY
		//     declaration of it, including the earlier one that carries no diagnostic of its own.
		//   • **By its own LINE**, for a declaration rejected on its merits — a `typealias Bad = Plain
		//     with Integer` over a non-generic base (E2055), a wrong arity (E2056), a bare or float type
		//     argument (E2061/E2062), a failed `where` constraint (E3017). Those anchor inside the RHS,
		//     not on the alias name, so only the line ties them back; and a typealias declaration is one
		//     line, so any diagnostic on it is about it.
		//
		// The reference reaches the same single-diagnostic shape structurally — its duplicate and RHS
		// checks THROW, so its end-of-parse unused check never runs — and v1 by an explicit
		// `constraintFailedTypealiases` set. shv2 needs the test because it reports these without
		// unwinding the parse; reading the diagnostics already recorded is what spares it a third
		// registry that every future declaration check would have to remember to write to.
		if project.collidedTypeNames.contains(fileScopedDeclKey(entry.name, declFilePath: entry.filePath)) or declarationAlreadyReported(project, entry: entry, upTo: alreadyReported) 'alreadyExplained'
			continue
		end 'alreadyExplained'

		// Scalars off the borrow, a fresh inline read for the managed halves — see the loop header's note.
		let line = entry.line
		let column = entry.column
		reportPositioned(project, code: ErrorCode.semanticUnusedTypeAlias, message: "unused typealias: '{String.from((try project.unusedTypeAliasDiagnostics.get(unusedIndex) otherwise panic("reportDeferredNameDiagnostics: unusedIndex {unusedIndex} is below project.unusedTypeAliasDiagnostics.count()")).name)}'", line: line, column: column, filePath: (try project.unusedTypeAliasDiagnostics.get(unusedIndex) otherwise panic("reportDeferredNameDiagnostics: unusedIndex {unusedIndex} is below project.unusedTypeAliasDiagnostics.count()")).filePath)
	end 'eachUnused'

	// An INDEX loop, not `for … in`: the element is a BORROW of `project`, and the call below is
	// recorded as writing that same storage. ⚖ Ruling of 2026-08-26 — canonical note at
	// `IR/Std/ElimTrivialBlockArgs.elimTrivialBlockArgs`.
	for hiddenIndex in 0 upto project.hiddenTypeNameDiagnostics.count() 'eachHidden'
		let line = (try project.hiddenTypeNameDiagnostics.get(hiddenIndex) otherwise panic("reportDeferredNameDiagnostics: hiddenIndex {hiddenIndex} is below project.hiddenTypeNameDiagnostics.count()")).line
		let column = (try project.hiddenTypeNameDiagnostics.get(hiddenIndex) otherwise panic("reportDeferredNameDiagnostics: hiddenIndex {hiddenIndex} is below project.hiddenTypeNameDiagnostics.count()")).column
		reportPositioned(project, code: ErrorCode.parserExpectedType, message: HiddenTypeNameMessage, line: line, column: column, filePath: (try project.hiddenTypeNameDiagnostics.get(hiddenIndex) otherwise panic("reportDeferredNameDiagnostics: hiddenIndex {hiddenIndex} is below project.hiddenTypeNameDiagnostics.count()")).filePath)
	end 'eachHidden'
end 'reportDeferredNameDiagnostics'

// Is there already a diagnostic against this declaration's own source line? See the caller for the
// rule; this is the scan that answers it.
//
// ⚠ It reads `project.diagnostics` DIRECTLY, and that is sound only because of WHERE the drain runs:
// every check that can refuse a typealias declaration — the parser's own artifact diagnostics, the
// merge's `commitTypeNameDecls`, and the pre-pipeline `checkConformance` / `checkGenericAliasCycles` /
// `checkWhereConstraints` / `checkTypeSymbolNamespace` — has already reported by the time the first
// pass runs. A check added LATER than `resolveTypes` would not be seen, which is the one thing to know
// before moving either end.
//
// The scan is linear in the diagnostics reported BEFORE the drain (`upTo`) and runs once per
// UNUSED-alias candidate — a population that is empty for every program that compiles, so a clean build
// never enters the loop.
//
// Paths compare by their own spelling rather than through `FilePath.equals`, which case-folds by
// cloning and lower-casing both sides on Windows. Every path here came out of `project.db.sourcePaths`,
// so two references to one file are literally the same string — the identical assumption
// `RangedAliasRegistry.multiplyDeclared` keys and probes on.
function declarationAlreadyReported(project Project, entry DeferredNameDiagnostic, upTo ElementIndex) returns bool
	for i in 0 upto upTo 'each'
		let reported = try project.diagnostics.get(i) otherwise panic("declarationAlreadyReported: i < upTo <= diagnostics.count() but get({i}) failed")
		if reported.line == entry.line and reported.filePath.toString().equals(entry.filePath.toString()) 'sameDeclaration'
			return true
		end 'sameDeclaration'
	end 'each'
	return false
end 'declarationAlreadyReported'

// ⭐ **THE FOURTH DECLARATION SITE — a FUNCTION TYPEALIAS's parameter and return types.** A
// `typealias Scaler = function(v Real) returns Real` names two types the three sweeps above never reach:
// the alias is not a function (so it has no `funcSignatures` entry to re-sync) and its types are stored
// interner-free (so nothing rewrote them at merge). This resolves them into `project.functionAliasShapes`,
// which is the form BOTH consumers need — the indirect call's argument widening and the function-type door
// agreement both ask about the REPRESENTATION, and a `named` alias answers neither until it is erased.
//
// ⚠ **IT RE-INTERNS THE STORED NAMES INTO `project.typeNames`** — the merged interner every id downstream of
// this pass lives in — because the alias deliberately carries names rather than ids (see FunctionTypeAlias).
// Interning is idempotent and the names came from files whose own interners already folded in, so this mints
// no new id in practice; it is what makes the alias's types comparable with a signature's at all.
//
// ⚠ Unlike `checkStructFieldTypes` it VALIDATES NOTHING. An undeclared name in a function alias's signature
// is already E3011 from the site that USES the alias (a parameter/field/return declared with it resolves
// through `resolveNamedType` on the way in); reporting it a second time here would double every such
// diagnostic. `resolveType`'s recovery answer for an unknown name is what lands in the shape, and the
// pipeline's error gate stops the build before anything reads it.
function resolveFunctionAliasShapes(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures)
	for (name, alias) in project.functionTypeAliases 'eachAlias'
		var resolvedParams = MaxonTypeArray.create()
		var preErasedNames = ByteArrayArray.create()
		for param in alias.paramTypes 'eachParam'
			// The reader is the alias's OWN declaring file — a function alias's signature is written there
			// and nowhere else, so a name it spells means what that file means by it.
			resolvedParams.push(resolveType(project, signatures: signatures, t: maxonTypeOfStoredAliasParam(param, typeNames: project.typeNames), readerFilePath: alias.declFilePath))
			// ⚠ **READ, NOT DERIVED — and it used to be derived, differently.** This tier once asked
			// `signatures.containsEnumInAnyFile(param.typeName)` while the declared-function tier asked
			// `Parser.aggregateNameOf`, so one parameter got two classifications: a per-instance ranged alias
			// carried an identity on one side and none on the other. The identity is now extracted ONCE, at the
			// parse, by the extraction both tiers fill through (`Parser.erasedAggregateNameOf`) — a shared
			// comparison over two different classifications would only have made the disagreement a refusal.
			preErasedNames.push(param.aggregateName)
		end 'eachParam'
		project.functionAliasShapes.upsert(name, value: FunctionShape.create(resolvedParams, returnType: resolveReturnType(project, signatures: signatures, ret: maxonTypeOfStoredAliasReturn(alias, typeNames: project.typeNames), readerFilePath: alias.declFilePath), paramAggregateNames: preErasedNames, returnAggregateName: alias.returnAggregateName))
	end 'eachAlias'
end 'resolveFunctionAliasShapes'

// ⭐ **THE RESOLVED SHAPE OF THE FUNCTION TYPE A GOVERNING NAME DENOTES — THE ONE LOOKUP.** A function value
// carries its type as `MaxonType.function(id)`, whose id names either the FUNCTION it refers to (a
// `functionRef`, or a lifted closure) or the function TYPEALIAS a declared place was written with — and
// "the declared type is authoritative" means those two must reach one answer through one door. A declared
// function's resolved shape already exists as `funcSignatures` + `funcReturnTypes` (`resolveTypes` re-synced
// both), so it is assembled on demand rather than duplicated into a third registry; an alias's was
// materialized by `resolveFunctionAliasShapes` because nothing else holds it.
//
// The two registries are DISJOINT by construction — `Parser.requireUnreservedName`/`checkTypeSymbolNamespace`
// keep a type name and a function name from colliding — so the order they are tried in cannot matter.
//
// `undeclared` is the honest answer for a name in neither, and every caller must handle it rather than assume.
union DeclaredFunctionShape
	declared(shape FunctionShape)
	undeclared
end 'DeclaredFunctionShape'

// The registries this reads travel as one `FunctionTypeRegistry` — see there for why all four move together.
function declaredFunctionShapeOf(registry FunctionTypeRegistry, name ByteArray) returns DeclaredFunctionShape
	if let sig = try registry.funcSignatures.get(name) 'declaredFn'
		let returnType = try registry.funcReturnTypes.get(name) otherwise panic("declaredFunctionShapeOf: '{String.from(name)}' is in funcSignatures but not funcReturnTypes — the two are committed together per function and re-synced together by resolveTypes")
		return DeclaredFunctionShape.declared(FunctionShape.create(sig.paramTypes, returnType: returnType, paramAggregateNames: sig.paramAggregateNames, returnAggregateName: sig.returnAggregateName))
	end 'declaredFn'

	if let aliasShape = try registry.aliasShapes.get(name) 'alias'
		return DeclaredFunctionShape.declared(aliasShape)
	end 'alias'

	return DeclaredFunctionShape.undeclared
end 'declaredFunctionShapeOf'

// The registry a `Project` denotes, assembled where a pass first needs one. It copies four references and
// nothing else, so it is not a cache and cannot go stale against the project it was taken from within a pass.
export function functionTypeRegistryOf(project Project) returns FunctionTypeRegistry
	return FunctionTypeRegistry.create(project.funcSignatures, funcReturnTypes: project.funcReturnTypes, aliasShapes: project.functionAliasShapes, typeNames: project.typeNames)
end 'functionTypeRegistryOf'

// ⭐ **DO TWO FUNCTION TYPES AGREE? THE ONE ANSWER, for every door a function value passes through.** Same
// arity, and position for position the same RESOLVED type — which is exactly what a call through the value
// depends on, because a call site derives its argument register files, its argument count and its result
// register from the type it was DECLARED with and gets whatever the callee actually is.
//
// It is deliberately NOT `typesAgree`, and the difference is the whole rule: `typesAgree` puts `int` and
// `float` in one class (`5 + 2.0` must compile), so it would accept `fn(Real) returns Integer` standing in
// for `fn(Real) returns Real` — the very silent wrong answer this exists to close. Nor is it the NOMINAL
// display comparison `checkValueMergeSignatures` makes; see FunctionShape for why a door and a merge ask
// different questions and get different rules.
export function functionShapesAgree(registry FunctionTypeRegistry, signatures ProgramSignatures, declared FunctionShape, got FunctionShape) returns bool
	return shapesAgreeUnder(registry, signatures: signatures, declared: declared, got: got, pending: sharedEmptyFunctionTypePairs)
end 'functionShapesAgree'

// The rule proper, carrying the pairs of function-type names already under comparison further down the
// same descent (see `FunctionTypePair`). Split from the entry point above only so the cycle guard is one
// argument the three call sites never have to know about.
function shapesAgreeUnder(registry FunctionTypeRegistry, signatures ProgramSignatures, declared FunctionShape, got FunctionShape, pending FunctionTypePairArray) returns bool
	if declared.paramTypes.count() != got.paramTypes.count() 'arity'
		return false
	end 'arity'

	for i in 0 upto declared.paramTypes.count() 'eachParam'
		let declaredParam = try declared.paramTypes.get(i) otherwise panic("shapesAgreeUnder: declared.paramTypes.get OOB at {i} — the count was just read from the same array")
		let gotParam = try got.paramTypes.get(i) otherwise panic("shapesAgreeUnder: got.paramTypes.get OOB at {i} — the arity check above proved the two counts equal")
		if not resolvedTypesMatch(registry, signatures: signatures, declared: declaredParam, got: gotParam, pending: pending) 'paramMismatch'
			return false
		end 'paramMismatch'
		// ⭐⭐ **AND THE PRE-ERASURE IDENTITY, WHICH `resolvedTypesMatch` STRUCTURALLY CANNOT ASK.** `resolveType`
		// collapses a boxed union/enum (and a per-instance ranged alias) to bare `integer`, so `function(Shade)`
		// and `function(Color)` are the SAME resolved parameter list and every enum agreed with every other.
		// **MEASURED, on programs that compiled clean and then died: SIGSEGV at a function-typed parameter,
		// `ACCESS_VIOLATION` at a struct field, `STATUS_STACK_OVERFLOW` at an erased RETURN** — the callee's
		// `match` indexing an ordinal past its own last arm.
		//
		// ⚠ **A CONJUNCTION WITH THE TAG RULE RATHER THAN A REPLACEMENT FOR IT, AND THAT IS SAFE BECAUSE THE TWO
		// ARE DISJOINT BY CONSTRUCTION.** The identity-first/tag-second shape `SemanticCheck.checkTransformParamType`
		// and `TypeRules.elementTypePreserved` are written in is the same rule stated for a caller holding ONE
		// side's live type; here both sides are already RESOLVED, so every tag whose identity survives resolution
		// (`structRef`, `interfaceRef`, `genericInstance`, `typeParameter`) is compared by id above while carrying
		// an EMPTY column, and every tag whose identity does not survive arrives as bare `integer` with nothing
		// for the tag rule to say. At most one of the two halves is informative about a position, so asking both
		// IS "identity where there is one, representation where there is not".
		//
		// ⚠ **THE EMPTY-AGAINST-NON-EMPTY CASE IS A DISAGREEMENT WHEREVER THE NAME IS AN AGGREGATE'S** —
		// `function(Integer)` reaching a `function(Color)` place would have the callee read an ordinal as a
		// number, or a number as an ordinal. `identitiesConflict` is the same rule every coercion site asks,
		// so a ranged alias — a scalar wearing a name — decays here exactly as it does there.
		if signatures.identitiesConflict(shapeParamIdentity(declared, index: i), got: shapeParamIdentity(got, index: i)) 'paramIdentityMismatch'
			return false
		end 'paramIdentityMismatch'
	end 'eachParam'

	// The RETURN slot asks both halves too, and for the same measurement — a shape carries ONE return identity
	// rather than a per-position array, because a function type returns exactly one thing.
	return returnSlotsMatch(registry, signatures: signatures, declared: declared.returnType, got: got.returnType, pending: pending) and not signatures.identitiesConflict(declared.returnAggregateName, got: got.returnAggregateName)
end 'shapesAgreeUnder'

// ⭐ **RENDER A RESOLVED SHAPE THE WAY IT WAS WRITTEN — the diagnostic twin of `functionShapesAgree`, and it
// exists for the reason `resolvedTypesMatch`'s nested arm states one screen up.** Rendering a shape's
// `paramTypes` directly is right for every position resolution preserves and WRONG for the ones it erases: a
// refusal over two different enums printed `expected 'fn(int) returns int', got 'fn(int) returns int'` — a
// diagnostic saying a type does not match itself, on the one refusal that most needs to be readable.
//
// ⚠ **IT UN-ERASES AND THEN DELEGATES, rather than re-implementing the `fn(…) returns …` assembly.** There is
// exactly ONE canonical spelling of a function type (`SignatureIndex.formatFnTypeDisplay`, which the merge
// check compares by name), and a second loop here would be a second spelling that could drift from it. So the
// only local work is putting each erased position's identity back as the `named` type it was before
// resolution, which is precisely what the shared renderer already knows how to print.
export function formatFunctionShapeDisplay(shape FunctionShape, typeNames TypeNameInterner, signatures ProgramSignatures) returns String
	var unerasedParams = MaxonTypeArray.create()
	for i in 0 upto shape.paramTypes.count() 'eachParam'
		unerasedParams.push(unerasedType(try shape.paramTypes.get(i) otherwise panic("formatFunctionShapeDisplay: paramTypes.get OOB at {i} — the count was just read from the same array"), identity: shapeParamIdentity(shape, index: i), typeNames: typeNames, signatures: signatures))
	end 'eachParam'

	let unerasedReturn = match shape.returnType 'r'
		void gives MaxonReturnType.void
		value(inner) gives MaxonReturnType.value(unerasedType(inner, identity: shape.returnAggregateName, typeNames: typeNames, signatures: signatures))
	end 'r'
	return formatFnTypeDisplay(unerasedParams, returnType: unerasedReturn, typeNames: typeNames, signatures: signatures)
end 'formatFunctionShapeDisplay'

// One position, with its pre-erasure identity put back. A position that carries none is already the type it
// was written as and passes through untouched.
//
// The name is looked up rather than interned, and it cannot be missing: a non-empty identity is a declared
// TYPE name, and this shape's own parameter reached `resolveNamedType` through that very name in
// `project.typeNames`. A miss therefore means a column was filled from an interner this renderer was not
// given, which is a compiler bug and never a program's.
// A shape caption spells a slot by its CLASS WORD — `fn(int) returns int` — so a ranged alias's identity
// (`Integer`) is not un-erased here: only a declared aggregate's is, because `int` would be a wrong
// caption for a `Color` and the identity is the only thing that still says so.
function unerasedType(t MaxonType, identity ByteArray, typeNames TypeNameInterner, signatures ProgramSignatures) returns MaxonType
	if identity.isEmpty() or signatures.isRangedAliasIdentity(identity) 'survivedResolution'
		return t
	end 'survivedResolution'

	return MaxonType.named(try typeNames.idOf(identity) otherwise panic("unerasedType: '{String.from(identity)}' is not in this interner — a pre-erasure identity is a declared type name, interned project-global by the merge that folded the file declaring it"))
end 'unerasedType'

// One position's entry in a shape's pre-erasure identity column. The two columns are built in lockstep with
// `paramTypes` at both fill sites, so an out-of-range read is a compiler bug rather than a program's.
export function shapeParamIdentity(shape FunctionShape, index ParamCount) returns ByteArray
	return try shape.paramAggregateNames.get(index) otherwise panic("shapeParamIdentity: paramAggregateNames.get OOB at {index} below count {shape.paramAggregateNames.count()} — a shape's parameter columns are built in lockstep with its {shape.paramTypes.count()} parameter type(s)")
end 'shapeParamIdentity'

// The pending list a top-level comparison starts from. ONE object for the whole compile and NEVER mutated
// (`pendingWithPair` builds a fresh array), the same pay-for-use discipline `sharedEmptyBorrowFacts` carries:
// a nested function type is rare, so the overwhelming majority of comparisons must allocate nothing at all.
let sharedEmptyFunctionTypePairs = FunctionTypePairArray.create()

// Two resolved RETURN slots are the same slot: both return nothing, or both return the same type. A `void`
// meeting a value (or the reverse) is a real disagreement — a call site that expects a result register from
// a function that writes none reads whatever was there.
function returnSlotsMatch(registry FunctionTypeRegistry, signatures ProgramSignatures, declared MaxonReturnType, got MaxonReturnType, pending FunctionTypePairArray) returns bool
	match declared 'declaredSlot'
		void then return isVoidReturnType(got)
		value(declaredValue) then return not isVoidReturnType(got) and resolvedTypesMatch(registry, signatures: signatures, declared: declaredValue, got: returnedValueType(got), pending: pending)
	end 'declaredSlot'
end 'returnSlotsMatch'

// Is this return slot the void arm?
function isVoidReturnType(ret MaxonReturnType) returns bool
	return match ret 'r'
		void gives true
		value gives false
	end 'r'
end 'isVoidReturnType'

// The type a non-void return slot carries. `void` is a caller bug — every caller asks `isVoidReturnType`
// first — so it panics rather than inventing a type nothing returns.
function returnedValueType(ret MaxonReturnType) returns MaxonType
	match ret 'r'
		value(inner) then return inner
		void then panic("returnedValueType: a `void` return slot carries no type — ask isVoidReturnType first")
	end 'r'
end 'returnedValueType'

// Two POST-RESOLUTION types are the same type: the same tag, and — for the tags that still carry an
// identity after resolution — the same id. Both sides' ids live in `project.typeNames` (a signature's were
// remapped project-global at merge, an alias shape's were interned there by `resolveFunctionAliasShapes`),
// so the ids are directly comparable.
//
// Which tags carry an identity is stated as an exhaustive match rather than a list of "interesting" ones,
// so a tag added to `ValueTypeTag` cannot default into "compare the tag only" and silently let two distinct
// types agree.
function resolvedTypesMatch(registry FunctionTypeRegistry, signatures ProgramSignatures, declared MaxonType, got MaxonType, pending FunctionTypePairArray) returns bool
	let declaredTag = maxonTypeTag(declared)
	if declaredTag != maxonTypeTag(got) 'differentTag'
		return false
	end 'differentTag'

	// ⭐ **A NESTED FUNCTION TYPE IS COMPARED BY SHAPE, exactly as the outer one is.** It is the same rule
	// one level down, and it has to be: `typealias Outer = function(f InnerA) returns Integer` accepting
	// `function runner(f InnerB)` is legal (both reference compilers accept it) when `InnerA` and `InnerB`
	// resolve alike, and REFUSING it is not merely strict — the diagnostic renders both sides as the same
	// string, so it reads as a type failing to match itself. The converse matters just as much: if the two
	// nested types genuinely differ, `runner`'s own body calls `f` through `InnerB`'s signature while the
	// caller supplied an `InnerA`, and the wrong answer lands one level in where nothing else looks.
	if declaredTag == ValueTypeTag.function 'nestedFunctionType'
		return nestedFunctionTypesAgree(registry, signatures: signatures, declaredId: typeNameIdOf(declared), gotId: typeNameIdOf(got), pending: pending)
	end 'nestedFunctionType'

	let identifiedByName = match declaredTag 'k'
		// Every one of these IS its own type: there is nothing else about an `int`, a `float`, a `bool`, an
		// `ExitCode`, a `void`, a `String` or a `Character` to disagree about.
		boolean to float gives false
		exitCode to void gives false
		string gives false
		// ⚠ A SEPARATE ARM, not `string to character`: the two are NOT adjacent — `function`,
		// `typeParameter` and `genericInstance` sit between them and every one of those `gives true`.
		// A range spanning them would silently flip three tags, which is the trap `ValueTypeTag`'s own
		// declaration warns about ("a range adopts whatever is inserted between its endpoints").
		character gives false
		// A `named` type and a struct are NOMINAL — two of them share a tag and are still different types.
		// (`named` should not survive resolution, which erases a ranged alias and a boxed union to
		// `integer`; comparing its id costs nothing and is the correct answer if one does.)
		named gives true
		structRef gives true
		// Handled above, by descent — a `function`-tagged type never reaches this table.
		function gives true
		// A type PARAMETER's id is a `(declaring type, parameter name)` DIGEST and a generic INSTANCE's is
		// its whole-program `GenericInstanceId`; two function types agree only if they take the same
		// instances and the same type parameters, so both are compared the same way for their own reasons.
		// ⚠ **THE PARAMETER HALF WAS ONLY APPROXIMATELY TRUE UNTIL W14**: with the id spelled as a POSITION,
		// `fn(T)` in one generic type and `fn(U)` in another compared EQUAL — a false ACCEPT between two
		// unrelated opaque types. The digest makes this arm correct by construction rather than by the two
		// sides happening to come from one declaration.
		typeParameter to genericInstance gives true
		// An EXISTENTIAL is nominal in its INTERFACE: a `Base`-typed place and a `Derived`-typed value are
		// two different types, so the ids must match. Compared for `named`/`structRef`'s reason.
		interfaceRef gives true
		// A `cstring` carries no name — every one of them is the same type, exactly as every `String` is.
		cstring gives false
	end 'k'

	return not identifiedByName or typeNameIdOf(declared) == typeNameIdOf(got)
end 'resolvedTypesMatch'

// Do the two function types these ids govern agree? The nested half of `functionShapesAgree`, and the same
// question, so it resolves both ids to shapes and asks that.
//
// The SAME id short-circuits to agreement before anything is looked up, which is what keeps this free on
// every ordinary program: a nested function parameter almost always spells the same alias on both sides, and
// that case allocates nothing and touches no registry. It is also what makes the cycle guard cheap — a
// SELF-referential alias meets itself here and never descends at all.
function nestedFunctionTypesAgree(registry FunctionTypeRegistry, signatures ProgramSignatures, declaredId TypeNameId, gotId TypeNameId, pending FunctionTypePairArray) returns bool
	if declaredId == gotId 'sameFunctionType'
		return true
	end 'sameFunctionType'

	let declaredName = functionTypeNameOf(registry, nameId: declaredId)
	let gotName = functionTypeNameOf(registry, nameId: gotId)
	if pairIsUnderComparison(pending, declaredName: declaredName, gotName: gotName) 'cycle'
		return true
	end 'cycle'

	// ⭐⭐ **A NESTED POSITION IS NOMINAL: `function(f Handler)` is not `function(f Callback)`.** A function
	// typealias names its own type, so once the two shapes agree the BRANDS still have to, and a nested
	// position is where the descent would otherwise erase the name it was written under.
	if signatures.functionBrandsConflict(declaredName, gotName: gotName) 'twoBrandsOfOneShape'
		return false
	end 'twoBrandsOfOneShape'

	return shapesAgreeUnder(registry, signatures: signatures, declared: requireFunctionShapeOf(registry, name: declaredName, side: "nested declared"), got: requireFunctionShapeOf(registry, name: gotName, side: "nested arriving"), pending: pendingWithPair(pending, declaredName: declaredName, gotName: gotName))
end 'nestedFunctionTypesAgree'

// ⭐ **THE NAME BEHIND A `function`-TAGGED TYPE'S ID — the ONE reader.** Every consumer of a function type
// holds an id (a value's `valueNameIds` entry, a declared `MaxonType.function`, a nested parameter) and needs
// the NAME, because that is what the registries are keyed by. All of them read it here: the ids are remapped
// project-global at merge, so one table answers for all three passes and none of them can resolve it its own
// way. SemanticCheck and LowerMaxonToStd each had a private copy of exactly this before.
export function functionTypeNameOf(registry FunctionTypeRegistry, nameId TypeNameId) returns ByteArray
	return try registry.typeNames.nameOf(nameId) otherwise panic("functionTypeNameOf: type-name id {nameId} is not in the project interner — a function type's name id is remapped project-global at merge (mergeArtifact)")
end 'functionTypeNameOf'

// ⭐ **THE SHAPE A GOVERNING NAME MUST HAVE — the ONE "it exists" wrapper**, for the four sites that require
// one: an indirect call's callee (asked once by SemanticCheck to check the arguments and once by the lowering
// to widen them), each end of a function-type door, and a function type nested inside another. `side` names
// which in the panic, so an invariant break says whether it was the callee, the place, the value or a nested
// parameter whose type went missing.
//
// It PANICS rather than reporting, and that is a statement about the parser rather than about the program:
// a `function` TAG is minted only from a name one of the three producers declares
// (`Parser.parseTypeReference` for a function alias, `emitFunctionRef` for a function,
// `parseClosureExpression` for a lifted closure), and every caller has already established that both its tags
// are `function`. A missing shape therefore means a producer was added without a registry entry, which is a
// compiler bug and never user input.
export function requireFunctionShapeOf(registry FunctionTypeRegistry, name ByteArray, side String) returns FunctionShape
	match declaredFunctionShapeOf(registry, name: name) 'shape'
		declared(shape) then return shape
		undeclared then panic("requireFunctionShapeOf: the {side} function type's governing name '{String.from(name)}' names neither a declared function/closure nor a function typealias — a `function` tag is minted only from one of the three (Parser.parseTypeReference / emitFunctionRef / parseClosureExpression)")
	end 'shape'
end 'requireFunctionShapeOf'

// Is this pair of names already being compared further up the same descent (see `FunctionTypePair`)? A linear
// scan, because the list holds one entry per level of function-type NESTING actually descended into — which
// is at most one on every program in the corpus and is bounded by the program's distinct function types.
function pairIsUnderComparison(pending FunctionTypePairArray, declaredName ByteArray, gotName ByteArray) returns bool
	for pair in pending 'eachPending'
		if pair.declaredName == declaredName and pair.gotName == gotName 'match'
			return true
		end 'match'
	end 'eachPending'
	return false
end 'pairIsUnderComparison'

// `pending` plus this pair, as a FRESH array — `sharedEmptyFunctionTypePairs` and every list above this one
// in the descent must stay untouched, because a sibling parameter compared after this one is not under it.
function pendingWithPair(pending FunctionTypePairArray, declaredName ByteArray, gotName ByteArray) returns FunctionTypePairArray
	var extended = FunctionTypePairArray.create()
	for pair in pending 'eachPending'
		extended.push(pair)
	end 'eachPending'
	extended.push(FunctionTypePair.create(declaredName, gotName: gotName))
	return extended
end 'pendingWithPair'

// ⭐ **THE THIRD DECLARATION SITE.** A program names a type in exactly three places — a PARAMETER,
// a RETURN type, and a FIELD — and until P1.1a wave 3 this pass walked only the first two. So a
// field declared `as Nonexistent` reached no check at all, and `Parser.fieldStorageType`'s recovery
// value became the FINAL answer: the program compiled clean and the field silently typed itself
// `i64`. A wrong answer, not a crash.
//
// It also made `fieldStorageType`'s own comment false. That comment justified its recovery value by
// pointing here — "E3011, which `TypeResolution` reports against the merged registry, the
// authority... Returning `integer` keeps the op well-formed for the instant before the pipeline's
// error gate throws" — and nothing was reporting it, so there was no instant and no gate. The
// comment described the right design; the implementation never had it. This walk is what makes the
// sentence true, which is why the sentence stayed.
//
// ⚠ **IT VALIDATES AND DOES NOT REWRITE**, and there are two independent reasons — either alone
// would be sufficient:
//   • **Nothing reads a resolved field type.** The parser cannot wait for this pass (an op's type
//     must be concrete when it is EMITTED, and `resolveTypes` never rewrites an op's type), so it
//     resolves a field's storage type itself, through the sweep. A rewrite here would be a
//     mechanism with no consumer.
//   • **The layout object is the ARTIFACT's own.** `commitStructTypes` upserts it by REFERENCE and
//     an artifact is MEMOIZED, so writing through it would mutate a cached parse — the hazard
//     IrBlock's header states for the phi model, reached from a different direction.
// So `resolveType`'s RESULT is discarded and its DIAGNOSTIC is the point. `resolveNamedType` stays
// the single place that knows what a named type denotes; asking it here rather than re-deriving
// "is this name declared?" is what keeps the three declaration sites answering to one authority.
//
// ⚠ **IT CANNOT LIVE AT THE COMMIT POINT**, which is the tempting alternative (`commitStructTypes`,
// where the layouts arrive in declaration order and a Map walk would not be needed).
// `mergeArtifact` folds ONE artifact at a time, so a field naming a type declared in a file folded
// LATER would report a false E3011. The registry is complete only once every artifact is in, and
// that is exactly here.
//
// The walk is over the registry MAP, so its order is the map's rather than the source's — the one
// place this pass's diagnostics are not in declaration order. It is deterministic (the same program
// hashes the same names into the same slots), which is what the byte-identical-output gate needs;
// and a program with two undeclared field types has two independent bugs, not an ordering.
function checkStructFieldTypes(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures)
	for (_, layout) in project.structTypes 'eachStruct'
		for fieldType in layout.fieldTypes 'eachField'
			_ = resolveType(project, signatures: signatures, t: fieldType, readerFilePath: layout.declFilePath)
		end 'eachField'
	end 'eachStruct'
end 'checkStructFieldTypes'

// Re-sync a function's `funcSignatures` entry with its now-resolved param types.
// The parser recorded the signature with the SAME raw param types it put on
// `func.maxonParamTypes` (both from one param-parse), so the resolved
// `func.maxonParamTypes` above is exactly the concrete type list to write back; the
// param NAMES are untouched (resolution never renames a parameter). A function with
// no registered signature (none at M5 — the parser records one per function it
// builds) is left alone rather than fabricated.
//
// All three pre-erasure name columns — `paramAggregateNames` (OPEN #54 Slice B2), its `returnAggregateName`
// twin (BATCH18-b) and `paramDeclaredNames` (the display spelling) — are carried STRAIGHT THROUGH. They are
// precisely what resolving `func.maxonParamTypes` destroys (a `named` union AND a `named` ranged alias both
// collapse to `integer` here), so they must be read off the SIGNATURE (which the parser captured before this
// pass) and never re-derived from the now-erased types. The two EXISTENTIAL columns (W58) ride through for the
// same reason and a stronger one: `instanceDenotedType` had already dropped the `with` arguments before this
// pass ever saw the type, so there is nothing here to re-derive them from at all.
function syncResolvedSignature(project Project, func IrFunction)
	if not project.funcSignatures.contains(func.name) 'noSig'
		return
	end 'noSig'
	let sig = try project.funcSignatures.get(func.name) otherwise panic("syncResolvedSignature: contains({String.from(func.name)}) true but get failed")
	project.funcSignatures.upsert(func.name, value: FuncSignature.create(sig.paramNames, paramTypes: sourceParamTypesOf(func, declaredCount: sig.paramNames.count()), paramAggregateNames: sig.paramAggregateNames, paramDeclaredNames: sig.paramDeclaredNames, returnAggregateName: sig.returnAggregateName, paramExistentialSites: sig.paramExistentialSites, returnExistentialSites: sig.returnExistentialSites, extensionConstraints: sig.extensionConstraints))
end 'syncResolvedSignature'

// ⭐ **THE FUNCTION'S SOURCE PARAMETER TYPES — WHICH IS NOT ALWAYS ALL OF `maxonParamTypes`.** A LIFTED
// CLOSURE carries one more (`Parser.parseClosureExpression` promotes the reserved `__env` slot to a real
// trailing parameter so every function value shares one indirect signature), and its `funcSignatures` entry
// deliberately records the USER parameters only. Copying `maxonParamTypes` wholesale therefore left a
// closure's signature with 2 types against 1 name — the very lockstep `checkArgTypes` panics about, held by
// every other function and silently broken by this one.
//
// It went unnoticed while nothing asked a signature how many parameters its TYPES describe: `slotCallArgs`
// counts NAMES, and a lifted closure has no direct call to slot. It stops being invisible the moment a
// function value's declared SHAPE is read off the signature — a closure then claims an arity one too high
// and a `fn(int, int) returns int` where the source wrote `fn(int)`.
//
// The NAME column is the authority for how many source parameters there are, and the types are prefix-
// aligned with it by construction (the env is APPENDED), so the fix is a prefix. A fresh array is built only
// when the counts differ, i.e. only for a lifted closure.
function sourceParamTypesOf(func IrFunction, declaredCount ParamCount) returns MaxonTypeArray
	if func.maxonParamTypes.count() == declaredCount 'alreadyExact'
		return func.maxonParamTypes
	end 'alreadyExact'

	var sourceParams = MaxonTypeArray.create()
	for i in 0 upto declaredCount 'eachDeclared'
		sourceParams.push(try func.maxonParamTypes.get(i) otherwise panic("sourceParamTypesOf: '{String.from(func.name)}' declares {declaredCount} parameter names but its type column holds only {func.maxonParamTypes.count()} — the compiler may APPEND a hidden parameter (a lifted closure's `__env`), never omit a declared one"))
	end 'eachDeclared'
	return sourceParams
end 'sourceParamTypesOf'

function resolveReturnType(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, ret MaxonReturnType, readerFilePath FilePath) returns MaxonReturnType
	return match ret 'r'
		void gives MaxonReturnType.void
		value(inner) gives MaxonReturnType.value(resolveType(project, signatures: signatures, t: inner, readerFilePath: readerFilePath))
	end 'r'
end 'resolveReturnType'

// ⚠ EXPORTED for `SemanticCheck.resolvedSlotThroughCallInstance` (F3), the one door outside this pass that
// asks it. A function shape SUBSTITUTED at a call site holds one slot this pass never saw — the instance's
// own type ARGUMENT, interned before the alias registries existed and so still a `named` LEAF — and it must
// be erased exactly as this pass erased every other slot of the shapes being compared. A second erasure
// written there would be a second answer to "what does this name denote", which is the disagreement this
// file's header is about.
export function resolveType(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, t MaxonType, readerFilePath FilePath) returns MaxonType
	match t 'r'
		boolean then return t
		integer then return t
		float then return t
		exitCode then return t
		named(id) then return resolveNamedType(project, signatures: signatures, id: id, readerFilePath: readerFilePath)
		unresolved then panic("resolveTypes: `unresolved` reached type resolution — the parser emits no unresolved placeholders at M1")
		// Already resolved, and by the PARSER: `Parser.parseTypeReference` mints a `structRef`
		// directly for `Self` (and for the enclosing type's own name) inside a `type` body, because
		// that mapping is SYNTACTIC — it needs the enclosing declaration and no registry, so the
		// token sweep and the real parse can both make it and cannot drift. This arm is the OTHER
		// half of that: what `resolveNamedType` produces below for a `type` named from outside its
		// own body, arriving back here through a second `resolveTypes` over a re-resolved signature
		// would find it idempotent. Nothing to do either way.
		structRef then return t
		// Already resolved, and by the PARSER: `Parser.parseTypeReference` mints `MaxonType.string`
		// directly for the `String` builtin (syntactically, like `Self` → `structRef`), so it never
		// arrives here as a `named` to resolve. This arm is what makes that concrete type idempotent
		// under a second `resolveTypes` — there is nothing to do.
		string then return t
		// Already resolved, and by the PARSER: `Parser.parseTypeReference`/`emitFunctionRef` mint
		// `MaxonType.function` directly (a function alias re-tagged, or a functionRef's own name), so it
		// never arrives as a `named` to resolve. Idempotent under a second `resolveTypes` — nothing to do.
		function then return t
		// Already concrete, and by the PARSER (P1.6-A): `Parser.parseTypeReference` mints a
		// `typeParameter` for a `T` in scope and a `genericInstance` for a generic-instance alias
		// reference, both directly. There is no substitution at this trivial slice — a `T` stays `T` (an
		// opaque i64) and an instance stays an instance (a struct pointer) — so resolution leaves both
		// untouched, exactly as it leaves `structRef`/`string`/`function`. The type parameter's payload is a
		// source-derived digest since W14, so there is nothing here a resolution could resolve.
		typeParameter then return t
		genericInstance then return t
		// Already concrete — `Parser.parseTypeReference` mints a `character` directly for the
		// `Character` builtin name, exactly as it mints `string` for `String`.
		character then return t
		// Already concrete — `Parser.parseTypeReference` mints an `interfaceRef` directly for a name the
		// whole-program interface registry knows, and `denotedNamedType` mints one for a swept `named` that
		// turns out to be an interface. Resolution leaves it untouched, as it leaves `structRef`.
		interfaceRef then return t
		cstring then return t
	end 'r'
end 'resolveType'

// ⭐ **THE TYPE NAMES THE COMPILER OWNS — a user declaration may not bind one (P1.7a slice 2b-ii).** Homed
// here because this is where the coupling lives: `resolveNamedType` below claims `ExitCode` and `HashValue`
// BEFORE it consults `structTypes`/`enumTypes`, and `createEnumRegistry` seeds `Ordering` INTO those
// registries — so under all three names the AUTHORITY's answer is fixed, while the PARSER's own `named`
// resolution (`resolveNamedStruct`, which reads the struct registry and knows nothing of these names) would
// happily follow a user declaration instead. That disagreement is not academic: **MEASURED — a user
// `type HashValue` made `Hashable.hash()`'s witness result a `structRef`, adopted as an owned struct box and
// dropped as one, faulting with 0xC0000005.** shv2 has no namespacing to keep a user's `Ordering` distinct
// from the builtin (the reference compilers do — theirs come from a stdlib MODULE), so the cure is to make
// the collision unrepresentable: `Parser.requireTypeNameNotCompilerOwned` refuses any declaration that would
// mint a NOMINAL identity under one of these names, which is what makes the arms below and the registry seed
// the SOLE writers of them. (A RANGED typealias over one is still legal — it erases to the same primitive
// the builtin does, so it cannot disagree; see that function.)
//
// ⭐ **`CharacterSet` AND `CharSet` JOIN THE LIST AT P1.8 SLICE D, AND THEIR SEED IS A DIFFERENT SHAPE —
// WHICH IS WHY THEY BELONG HERE RATHER THAN IN A CHECK OF THEIR OWN.** The three above are seeded by
// `resolveNamedType`'s arms and by `createEnumRegistry`; these two are seeded by a DECLARATION —
// `stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon`'s own `type CharacterSet` and `typealias CharSet = Set with Character`, the
// second of which fills the `CharSet` entry of the SAME `genericAliases` map a user `typealias` folds into.
// (The compiler wrote its own unconditional upsert of that entry until W129; the seed moved, the reservation
// did not.) A map entry is OVERWRITTEN rather than contested, so
// before this line a user declaration was not refused — it was silently taken, and the author was
// then blamed at the first USE of the name the compiler had taken from them. MEASURED, on
// `typealias CharSet = Set with String` + `s.insert("a")`:
// *"`Set with Character` requires a Character key — got a 'String' value"*, reported at line 5 for a
// declaration written on line 1. The rule the author broke is stated on line 1, so that is where it is
// now reported.
//
// ⚠ **THE RANGED CARVE-OUT IS INHERITED UNCHANGED, AND IT IS SOUND FOR THESE TWO FOR A DIFFERENT REASON.**
// A `typealias CharSet = int(0 to 5)` carries a nominal identity of its own, and it still cannot contest
// the seed, because a RANGED alias and a GENERIC-INSTANCE alias live in different registries and are
// reached by different syntax: `CharSet from ['x']` asks `genericAliases`, a ranged reference asks the
// alias registry. MEASURED: both readings coexist and each answers for its own syntax (a
// `CharSet from ['x']` still builds the member set, `true`, exit 0). So the argument here is a
// DISJOINTNESS one rather than the erasure one above, and the answer is the same — legal.
// ⭐ **`Codepoint` JOINS THE LIST AT P1.8 SLICE E, AND IT JOINS FOR `HashValue`'S EXACT REASON.** It is the
// second name `isSynthesizedIntAliasName` erases to `integer` without a registry entry, so
// `denotedNamedType`'s synthesized-alias arm answers for it BEFORE `containsStruct` is consulted — which is
// precisely the disagreement the `HashValue` measurement records: the AUTHORITY would erase a user
// `type Codepoint` to `integer` while the PARSER's own `named` resolution followed the declaration and
// tagged the value `structRef`. Adding the name to `isSynthesizedIntAliasName` without adding it here would
// have re-opened that hole under a new name.
//
// The RANGED carve-out applies to it unchanged and is what the corpus needs: a user
// `typealias Codepoint = int(0 to 1114111)` — the very declaration `stdlib/Character.maxon` makes — erases
// to the same `integer` the builtin does, so the two answers agree and there is nothing to refuse.
//
// ⭐⭐ **`ArrayError` JOINS THE LIST AT R4.4's REVIEW, AND IT JOINS FOR `Ordering`'S EXACT REASON — IT IS
// SEEDED BY `createEnumRegistry` UNDER A BARE NAME.** R4.4 registered a THIRD compiler-owned enum there
// (`Project.builtinArrayErrorEnum`) and could not give it the `__` prefix its two `__ManagedMemoryError` /
// `__ManagedFileError` siblings wear, because user source already spells the bare name in a `throws` clause
// (see `SignatureIndex.ArrayErrorTypeName`). A bare seed and no entry here is precisely the hole this
// function's header describes, and it was not hypothetical.
// ⚠ **MEASURED, before this line existed:** a program declaring `enum ArrayError` with cases
// `somethingElse`/`another` COMPILED, `upsertDeclaredEnum` let the declaration displace the seed, and
// `try arr.get(5) otherwise (e) … match e` routed the runtime's `indexOutOfBounds` (ordinal 0) into the
// user's `somethingElse` arm — exit 11, no diagnostic anywhere. One error type under two meanings, decided
// by whichever write landed last: the wrong answer the seed is supposed to be undisplaceable against.
// ⇒ The reservation is only over a DECLARATION. `throws ArrayError` is a type REFERENCE and stays legal —
// which is the whole reason the name is bare — and the four committed cases that write it are unaffected.
//
// ⭐⭐ **`String` AND `Character` JOIN THE LIST AT BATCH2 SLICE 2, AND THEY JOIN FOR A REASON NONE OF THE
// SEVEN ABOVE HAS: THEIR COLLISION IS OVER A *SYMBOL*, NOT OVER A TYPE.** Every name above collides because
// two answers to *"what does this name denote?"* disagree. These two denote the builtin under every syntax
// there is — `Parser.parseTypeReference` settles both SYNTACTICALLY, so a user `type String` can never be
// named at a parameter, `String{…}` is E3076 and `String.create(…)` is E2015 at the member
// (`Parser.requireCompilerOwnedStaticIsResolvable`, which took that refusal over from the
// `parseStringStaticCall` door W55 retired) —
// which made the declaration look harmless, and is exactly why the hole survived. What it captures is the
// METHOD SYMBOL: `Parser.declaredMethodName` joins its `hash()` into `String.hash`, the very bytes
// `qualifiedMethodName(StringConformerName, HashMethodName)` spells for the impl
// `BuiltinConformanceRuntime` synthesizes — and that installer builds an impl only when the module does not
// already DEFINE the name (`undefinedImplNames`), so the user's inert declaration does not collide with the
// builtin, it silently REPLACES it.
//
// ⚠⚠ **TWO WRONG ANSWERS, BOTH MEASURED ON THE UNCHANGED TREE, NEITHER WITH ANY DIAGNOSTIC ANYWHERE:**
//   • `Box uses T where T is Hashable`, `Box with String`, `.itemHash()` of `""` returned **7** — the user's
//     `hash()` body — where djb2 answers **5381**. The `__witness_String.Hashable` slot was stamped with a
//     `funcAbs64` reloc naming `String.hash`, and the user owned that symbol.
//   • `Set with String` probes through the same two slots, so `insert("alice"); insert("bob");
//     insert("alice")` counted **3** against a control's **2** — a duplicate key stored twice, because the
//     user's `equals` answered `false`.
// A third door — the direct `s.hash()` / `s.equals(t)` dispatch — opened in the same rung, which is what
// forced the fact to be enforced rather than merely asserted: the tree already SAID in prose that `String`
// and `Character` are names the compiler owns, while nothing checked it.
// `IrInterface.isBuiltinConformanceImplName` now DEPENDS on this line: it declares every `String.`-qualified
// callee to be the compiler's own, which is true only while no USER declaration can mint one.
//
// ⛔⛔ **AND THAT LAST WORD IS THE WHOLE OF IT — THE RESERVATION IS OVER *WHOSE* DECLARATION, NOT OVER
// WHETHER ONE EXISTS. THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO SAY "…which is why neither `stdlib/String.maxon` nor
// `stdlib/Character.maxon` can EVER be loaded", AND THAT CLAUSE WAS FALSE (W16).** It described shv2's
// scaffolding as if it were a law about the language, and **both reference compilers disprove it**: the C#
// bootstrap and v1 each hold `String` as a builtin AND compile `stdlib/String.maxon` — indeed each takes
// String's whole LAYOUT from that file and neither has a name-reservation list at all.
// ⚠ **WHAT THE CLAUSE ACTUALLY DESCRIBED WAS A BUG, AND IT WAS COSTING TWO REACHABLE COMPILER PANICS.**
// `Parser.requireTypeNameNotCompilerOwned` admits a stdlib file, so the real parse walked into `type String`'s
// body — while `ProgramSignatures.recordStruct` was dropping EVERY declaration of such a name, the corpus's
// included, and the parser then found no layout for the declaration it stood inside. MEASURED before the fix:
// `maxon-shv2 build stdlib/String.maxon` panicked in `enclosingLayout` and `.../Character.maxon` in
// `requireConstructible`. Giving that one write the provenance bit `upsertDeclaredEnum` already had cures
// both; see `SignatureIndex.recordStruct` for the measurement.
// ⇒ ✅ **BOTH MODULES LOAD TODAY** — the loader takes all of `stdlib/` — and the blocker the sentence here
// named as remaining was never this rule but the REPRESENTATION: shv2 keeps String's own record
// (`Runtime/StringRuntime.StringRecordBytes`) while the corpus declares fields, so a corpus
// `String{managed: …}` could build against offsets the builtin does not read. That is now held to ONE
// description by `ProgramSignatures.checkFusedWrapperRecordLayouts`, which reads the corpus declaration and
// the runtime's offsets together because both are always present. The reservation above is unaffected: it
// is about WHOSE declaration may mint the name, not about whether the corpus declares it.
//
// ⚠ Neither name costs a program anything it could use. `Character` is the same case in every particular,
// including the same two synthesized impls under `Character.hash`/`Character.equals`; it is reserved with
// `String` rather than after the next measurement, because the two are one fact and finding out which of a
// pair is enforced by measuring the other is how this list grew in the first place.
// ⛔⛔ **`StringError` AND `MapError` ARE NOT ON THIS LIST, AND THEY ARE THE CASE THAT SHOWS A RESERVATION
// CAN OUTLIVE ITS REASON.** Both joined for `ArrayError`'s — each was SEEDED by `createEnumRegistry` under a
// bare name, and a bare seed a user declaration can displace is the hole the paragraph above measured. Both
// seeds are gone: `stdlib/String.maxon:6` and `stdlib/Map.maxon:6` are the declarations now, no runtime
// transcribes either enum's ordinals (`Compiler.compileToCodeResult` retired both claims at W49 wave 3 and
// W105), and `Project.builtinStringErrorEnum`/`builtinMapErrorEnum` no longer exist. What was left was a
// reservation with nothing behind it, refusing an ordinary program — `enum StringError … end` in a user file,
// which the runnable oracle compiles and runs — for a collision the compiler can no longer have.
// ⇒ **A LIBRARY NOMINAL NAME A USER CONTESTS IS ALREADY ANSWERED, AND NOT BY THIS LIST**:
// `SignatureIndex.contestStdlibTypeName` moves the library's declaration to `__Name`, which is the third of
// `Project.typeNamePairMayCoexist`'s four coexistence routes. That door SKIPS a compiler-owned name, so an
// entry here is what turns "shadow the library's" into "refuse the user's" — a name belongs here only while
// something OTHER than the declaration reads its ordinals or its identity.
// ⭐ **`IterationError` IS ON THE LIST FOR `ArrayError`'S REASON**, and its stake is the plainest of the four: the ordinals
// under this name are the ones a user's OWN `throw IterationError.exhausted` and the `match` that catches it
// are both resolved against, so a displaceable entry would let one program hold two numberings of one enum.
//
// ⭐⭐ **`StringIndex` JOINS AT W49 WAVE 3, AND IT IS THE FIRST NAME THAT ARRIVED BY GIVING ONE UP.** shv2
// held its own layout for that type under the RESERVED spelling `__StringIndex`, whose whole argument was
// this one — *"a user program declaring its own `type StringIndex` would land in the same bucket, and
// whichever write ran last would win"*. The reservation was right and the second declaration was not: it
// made the corpus's `charAt(idx StringIndex)` unsatisfiable (`E3005 … expected 'StringIndex', got
// '__StringIndex'`) by a value any program could actually produce. Wave 3 retires the producers onto the
// corpus and the bare name becomes the one declaration — so the argument moves HERE, where `String` and
// `Character`'s already live, and buys the identical property with no second layout behind it. MEASURED
// before the move: `type StringIndex  var a as int  end` COMPILED, and `specs-shv2/string-index.md`'s
// `error.a-user-may-not-declare-the-string-index-type` is that program.
//
// ⛔⛔ **`CharacterError` JOINS AT W135's REVIEW, AND IT IS THE ONE THAT SHOWS THE MEMBERSHIP TEST IS NOT
// "IS IT SEEDED?".** Every paragraph above reaches this list through a SEED — a bare name
// `createEnumRegistry` writes, which a user declaration could displace. This name is seeded by nothing:
// `stdlib/Character.maxon:15-17` is its only declaration. It belongs anyway, because the property the list
// actually buys is *one program holds ONE numbering of one enum*, and a seed is merely the commonest way to
// end up with two. Here the second numbering comes from the RUNTIME:
// `Runtime/GraphemeRuntime.buildCharAscii` returns `notAscii`'s ordinal as `__char_ascii`'s error flag, so a
// user `enum CharacterError` displacing the corpus one in `upsertDeclaredEnum` leaves the runtime emitting 0
// into whichever case that user wrote first — `IterationError`'s stake, arrived at from the other direction.
// MEASURED: that program compiled clean before this entry, and `specs-shv2/character-type.md`'s
// `error.declare-character-error-enum` is it.
//
// ⭐⭐ **AND TWO OF THESE NAMES ARE NO LONGER SEEDED AT ALL — THEY ARE READ FROM THE CORPUS (W3).**
// `Ordering` and `IterationError` are DECLARED by `stdlib/Interfaces.maxon`, which the loader now lists, so
// what they hold is the corpus's own layout rather than a transcription of it (`Project.createEnumRegistry`,
// `Project.requireCorpusDeclaredEnum`). **That does not take them off this list, and the distinction is what
// the list is FOR**: `Project.upsertDeclaredEnum` admits a compiler-owned name from the CORPUS and drops it
// from a USER file, so this function is what tells the two apart — a reservation over *whose* declaration,
// not over whether one exists. `HashValue` is the same shape one registry over: `stdlib/Interfaces.maxon:6`
// declares the ranged alias and its bounds are now the program's, while the name stays reserved here.
export function isCompilerOwnedTypeName(name ByteArray) returns bool
	return name.equals(ExitCodeBuiltinName) or name.equals(HashValueTypeName) or name.equals(CodepointTypeName) or name.equals(OrderingTypeName) or name.equals(CharacterSetBuiltinName) or name.equals(CharacterMemberSetAliasName) or name.equals(ArrayErrorTypeName) or name.equals(IterationErrorTypeName) or name.equals(CharacterErrorTypeName) or name.equals(StringConformerName) or name.equals(CharacterConformerName) or name.equals(StringIndexTypeName)
end 'isCompilerOwnedTypeName'

// The name a `named` type denotes, ERASED to a concrete `MaxonType` — or `notDeclared` when it
// denotes nothing. THE one list of the sources a named type can come from; see this file's header
// for its three readers and for why it reads the signature index rather than `Project`.
//
// It THROWS rather than answering with a union case, and the reason is measured rather than
// stylistic: a Maxon union with an associated value is heap-boxed, this is asked once per named type
// reference in the whole program, and `RangedAliasRegistry.underlyingInAnyFile` already records what
// one such box per reference cost (+36 × 2^n allocations across the scale ladder — a term that grows
// with the PROGRAM, where every other cost of alias resolution grows only with the DECLARATIONS).
// `UnknownTypeName` is payload-free, so it is a scalar; the caller already holds the name.
// ⭐⭐ **`readerFilePath` IS WHICH FILE IS ASKING, AND IT IS NOT DECORATION.** Two declarations of one name
// may now stand at once when neither can see the other (`typeNamePairMayCoexist`), and at that point the
// three NOMINAL arms below and the alias arms beneath them are BOTH right — for different files. Without
// the reader, the arm ORDER decided it for the whole program: MEASURED, `stdlib/Builtins.maxon`'s own
// `i64.min as ParsedInt` resolved to a USER program's `type ParsedInt` and the E3009 named a library line
// the author never opened. The reader for a stored DECLARATION's type is its DECLARING file, never the
// caller's — see `resolveNamedType`, which is where each of the three is obtained.
export function denotedNamedType(signatures ProgramSignatures, name ByteArray, id TypeNameId, readerFilePath FilePath) returns MaxonType throws UnknownTypeName
	// ⭐⭐ **THE NAME *AND* THE ANSWER ARE `builtinTypeNameTag`'S — THIS ARM OWNS NEITHER (W1 review).** This
	// used to spell `ExitCode` and `MaxonType.exitCode` itself, which made the AUTHORITY for what a type name
	// denotes and the TABLE the interner-free doors re-derive from two independent lists of which builtin
	// names carry a sub-64 width. They agreed; nothing made them. Adding the next such name here — the
	// natural place, since without an arm here the name is E3011 everywhere — and forgetting the table is
	// precisely the arrangement that dropped `ExitCode`'s width at the function-typealias door: silent on
	// every register target, a `wasm trap: indirect call type mismatch` on wasm. Sourced from the table, the
	// omission cannot be made in that direction at all; in the other direction (table, not here) the name is
	// E3011, which is loud.
	//
	// ⚠ **ONLY `exitCode` IS ADMITTED, and the bound is MEASURED rather than cautious.** The table also
	// answers for `bool`/`int`/`float`/`String`, and those answers are correct at the INTERFACE door (which
	// asks about a RENDERED type name) and WRONG here (which asks what a `named` REFERENCE denotes):
	// `bool`/`int`/`float` are keywords and `String`/`Character` are settled syntactically by
	// `Parser.parseTypeReference`, so a `named` reference never carries one of those names from a type
	// position at all.
	//
	// ⛔ **THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO REST ON A PROGRAM THAT CAN NO LONGER BE WRITTEN, AND W16 RE-MEASURED IT.**
	// It argued the bound from a user `enum String` — "`String` is deliberately not in
	// `isCompilerOwnedTypeName`", so `Self` inside such an enum arrives here as `named("String")` and
	// admitting the table's `string` answer would resolve it to the managed builtin instead of its i64 tag —
	// and it carried a MEASUREMENT saying that enum "compiles and runs on the host and on wasm". **Both
	// halves went stale at BATCH2 slice 2**, which added `String` and `Character` to
	// `isCompilerOwnedTypeName` for the method-SYMBOL capture the function's own header records. RE-MEASURED
	// at W16 on this tree: that exact program — `enum String` with an `export function flip() returns Self` —
	// is now **E2015** (`Parser.requireTypeNameNotCompilerOwned`, which every nominal declaration door asks),
	// so there is no user declaration left that can route one of these names into a `named` reference.
	// ⇒ The BOUND is unchanged and is now the stronger of the two arguments standing alone: a name settled
	// syntactically never reaches this door, so admitting it here could only ever answer for a reference that
	// cannot arrive. The stale copy is kept visible rather than deleted because it is the second time this
	// file's own header has had to record that a reservation moved and a justification elsewhere did not.
	let builtinTag = builtinTypeNameTag(name)
	if builtinTag == ValueTypeTag.exitCode 'isExitCode'
		return maxonTypeOfTag(builtinTag, nameId: id)
	end 'isExitCode'

	// ⭐ A COMPILER-SYNTHESIZED RANGED INT ALIAS — `HashValue`, the result type of `Hashable.hash()`. It is in
	// no alias registry (shv2 reads no stdlib module that declares it, so its
	// `typealias HashValue = int(0 to u32.max)` is in no registry), and without this arm it is E3011 on
	// every user `hash() returns HashValue`. It
	// resolves to width-free `integer` exactly as a ranged int alias does (`alias.underlying` drops the
	// range) — the u32 mask lives in the synthesized `int.hash` body, not the type. Handled inline like
	// `ExitCode` rather than seeded into `typeAliases`, which `resetMergeTargets` recreates empty per
	// warm-rebuild miss.
	//
	// The NAME SET is `isSynthesizedIntAliasName`'s, not this arm's, because `Parser.rangedAliasTag` reduces
	// the same names at parse time and the two answered differently until it was named once — see that
	// function's header for the `i.hash().hash()` this cost.
	if isSynthesizedIntAliasName(name) 'isSynthesizedIntAlias'
		return MaxonType.integer
	end 'isSynthesizedIntAlias'

	// ⭐ A DECLARED `type` KEEPS ITS NAME ID, and that one line is the difference between the two
	// registries this function consults. An alias RESOLVES AWAY — `Score` becomes `int`, and nothing
	// DOWNSTREAM OF THIS PASS can tell it was ever written; its identity is spent before resolution, at
	// the coercion doors. A struct's is needed after: `p.x` needs to know WHICH type
	// `p` is to find `x`'s slot (`Parser.emitFieldLoad` reads the id back out and asks
	// `structOf`), and two structs with identical fields are still two types. So the id survives,
	// carried by `structRef`.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **IT USED TO BE TESTED BEFORE THE ALIAS REGISTRY "only for readability", ON THE CLAIM THAT A NAME
	// CANNOT BE BOTH — and that claim was already false when it was written.** A `typealias Box` and a
	// `type Box` of one name IS diagnosed (E3006, `TypeNameRegistry`), so the paragraph that stood here was
	// stale in one direction; and since `typeNamePairMayCoexist` a pair whose two declarations cannot see
	// each other is LEGAL, so it is stale in the other. The order is therefore load-bearing after all, and
	// what makes it right is the guard rather than the position: a nominal arm claims a contested name only
	// for a reader the nominal declaration actually answers for. `crossKindContested` is an empty-map probe
	// for every program that contests nothing, so the hot path is what it was.
	//
	// `containsStruct`, not `structOf`: the layout itself is not wanted here, and `structOf` boxes a
	// throwaway `DeclaredStruct.undeclared` for the COMMON no answer. Resolution answers WHICH TYPE
	// this name is, and the answer is the id the reference already carries. The fields are the
	// parser's question, asked of the signature index at the use site.
	if signatures.containsStructInAnyFile(name) and not signatures.aliasShadowsNominalFor(name, readerFilePath: readerFilePath) 'isStruct'
		return MaxonType.structRef(id)
	end 'isStruct'

	// ⭐⭐ **A DECLARED `interface` KEEPS ITS NAME ID TOO, and for the struct arm's reason one level up
	// (P1.7a-existentials slice 1).** A value held at an interface type is nothing BUT a nominal
	// distinction: `b.run()` needs to know WHICH interface `b` is held at to number its witness slot, and
	// two interfaces declaring identical methods are still two types with two witness tables. So the id
	// survives, carried by `interfaceRef`.
	//
	// ⚠ **THIS ARM IS WHAT REPLACES THE INTEGER FALLBACK, AND THE FALLBACK WAS THE BUG.** Without it the
	// cascade fell through to its recovery `MaxonType.integer` — so an interface-typed field or parameter
	// resolved to an INT, `TypeRules.typeClassOf` read it as numeric and `typeTagName` printed it `int`.
	// MEASURED: a field declared `as Backend` was refused *"E3005: cannot assign 'struct' to variable 'b'
	// of type 'int'"* for an interface declared three lines above, and a method call on such a parameter
	// was *"E3011: Unknown type 'Backend'"* — a message that was false about a name the program declares.
	//
	// It is asked of `interfaceMap()`, the SAME whole-program store `Parser.parseTypeReference` and every
	// witness resolution read, so the AUTHORITY and the parser cannot come to disagree about which names
	// are interfaces. `lookupInterface` (not a bare map probe) is what admits the SYNTHESIZED protocol
	// interfaces on the same footing as the user-declared ones — the one lookup rule, stated in
	// `IrInterface`.
	// The contest guard is the struct arm's, for the struct arm's reason — a user `interface Tolerance`
	// beside `stdlib/Testing.maxon`'s `typealias Tolerance` is exactly the same shape one keyword over. The
	// membership is read into a bool first because a `match` arm is ONE statement and this arm has to be
	// able to FALL THROUGH to the alias arms below rather than answer.
	if signatures.containsInterfaceFor(name, readerFilePath: readerFilePath) 'anInterfaceAnswersHere'
		return MaxonType.interfaceRef(id)
	end 'anInterfaceAnswersHere'

	// ⭐ A DECLARED `enum`/`union` RESOLVES AWAY TO `integer`, the OPPOSITE of a struct — and for the
	// same reason an alias does. An enum value IS its i64 tag (int backing) or its float BIT PATTERN
	// (float backing), never a heap box, so the type carries no nominal identity downstream: after
	// resolution nothing needs to know a value was a `Color` rather than a bare int, because the
	// only thing that inspects an enum — `match` — reads the enum by NAME from the whole-program
	// signature index at PARSE time, long before this pass runs. So the id is dropped, exactly as a
	// ranged alias's is. (Float-backed enums resolve to `integer` too: the bits ARE an i64, matched
	// by an i64 compare — see EnumLayout.)
	//
	// The compiler-known `Ordering` resolves HERE rather than through an inline arm of its own, because
	// `createEnumRegistry` seeds it INTO this registry — one registration, every reader, no arm to add.
	// The contest guard is the struct arm's — and this arm needs it even though its ANSWER is the same
	// `integer` a ranged int alias gives: a user `enum RepeatCount` beside `stdlib/Builtins.maxon`'s
	// `typealias RepeatCount` erases alike, but a FLOAT alias does not, and neither does a generic or
	// function one. An arm that was right by coincidence for one alias form is not right.
	if signatures.containsEnumInAnyFile(name) and not signatures.aliasShadowsNominalFor(name, readerFilePath: readerFilePath) 'isEnum'
		return MaxonType.integer
	end 'isEnum'

	// A PLAIN ranged alias ("Score") — checked FIRST after the aggregates, and BEFORE the qualified-alias
	// path below, so the common case never pays the qualified split. A plain alias resolves to its underlying
	// primitive: the NAME and the RANGE are both erased here, and both were already spent — the name at the
	// coercion doors, the range at the guards `InsertRangeChecks` plans.
	//
	// ⚠ Bare-name (`underlyingInAnyFile`), and that is exactly right HERE rather than a gap: this arm
	// wants only `underlying`, and it DROPS the range — which is the erasure the whole type system
	// rests on. The RANGE is resolved per declaring file, where it is actually enforced
	// (`InsertRangeChecks`). A file to scope by is also genuinely absent on one of the two paths in:
	// `checkStructFieldTypes` resolves a `StructLayout`'s field types, and a layout records no
	// declaring file. This is the HOTTEST reader of the registry — once per named type reference in
	// the program — which is why the door it asks through hands back the primitive rather than a
	// `DeclaredAlias` that would be heap-boxed per call.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND IT ANSWERS ONLY WHERE A *RANGED* ALIAS IS WHAT THE READER MEANS.** The registry is bare and
	// whole-program, so for a name two files declare in two FORMS it answered for both — which is how
	// `a.maxon`'s own `5 as Handler`, whose only `Handler` is an `int` alias, came to be refused *"Cannot
	// cast from int to function"*. A reader that means the FUNCTION or GENERIC form has had its reference
	// re-tagged by `Parser.parseTypeReference` long before this door, so declining here loses no answer; it
	// is this door refusing to hand the other form's reader a meaning that is not its.
	if let underlying = try signatures.aliasUnderlyingInAnyFile(name) 'isPlainAlias'
		if signatures.readerMeansKind(name, readerFilePath: readerFilePath, kind: TypeDeclKind.rangedAlias) 'itIsTheReadersOwnForm'
			return underlying
		end 'itIsTheReadersOwnForm'
	end 'isPlainAlias'

	// ⭐ A QUALIFIED ALIAS (P1.6-C) — a base-template inner alias ("Wrapper.Idx", the shared-body form a
	// generic method's `Idx` carries) OR a per-instance alias ("IntWrapper.Idx"). Both ERASE to their
	// underlying primitive here, EXACTLY as a plain ranged alias does — so `maxonTypeToStdType` never sees a
	// `named` residue in a signature or field type. The per-instance one's nominal name survives only in the
	// VALUE column, where the front-end identity checks read it; a signature carries no identity. Reached only
	// for a name that is NEITHER a plain alias nor an aggregate — a rare, dotted name — so the qualified-name
	// split never touches the plain-alias hot path.
	// ⚠ It is asked from the READER's file, not `CompilerOwnedDeclFilePath`. It used to pass the latter on the
	// grounds that "this arm wants only `underlying`" and that a `StructLayout`'s field types arrive with no
	// declaring file — the second half of which is no longer true (`checkStructFieldTypes` reads
	// `layout.declFilePath`), and the first was never a reason to hand a per-instance lookup the wrong file.
	match signatures.qualifiedAliasOf(name, readerFilePath: readerFilePath) 'qualified'
		declared(alias) then return alias.underlying
		undeclared then break 'qualified'
	end 'qualified'

	// A name in NONE of the sources is a USER error — a typo (`Scor` for `Score`), a bare
	// sized type (`u8` is not a type in Maxon; a ranged alias over it is), or a
	// user-defined type this slice does not have. Each reader decides what to DO about it:
	// this pass reports E3011 unpositioned and recovers, the two cast sites raise the same
	// code anchored on the `as` they were written at.
	//
	// ⚠ **ONE ARRIVAL HERE IS NOT A TYPO, AND ITS NOUN IS WRONG: a CROSS-KIND CONTESTED name whose reader
	// means a NON-RANGED alias form.** Such a reference is re-tagged by `Parser.parseTypeReference` long
	// before this door — the whole reason the ranged arm above declines it — so the only way one reaches
	// this line is through a caller that resolves a type WITHOUT that re-tag, and "Unknown type 'Handler'"
	// is then false about a name the program declares twice over. It is left rather than papered over: the
	// honest text is a sentence about a name meaning something else HERE, which is a diagnostic this
	// registry has no code for, and inventing one is not this change's to do. Reported to the coordinator
	// with that reasoning rather than guessed at.
	throw UnknownTypeName.notDeclared
end 'denotedNamedType'

// The one wording of "this name denotes no type", shared by the anchorless declaration sites below
// and by every POSITIONED site in the parser — the two `as` casts and the three member-access doors,
// all through `Queries.reportParseError`'s one `unknownTypeName` arm — so one fact has one text
// however it is reached.
export function unknownTypeMessage(typeName String) returns String
	return "Unknown type '{typeName}'"
end 'unknownTypeMessage'

// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO SENTENCES FOR A BARE `int`/`float`, IN THE ORACLE'S OWN WORDING, BYTE FOR BYTE**
// (`maxon-sharp/Compiler/2-Parser.cs:9146-9158`). Transcribed rather than improved: the specs compare
// stderr exactly, and the user ruling for this rule was to match the oracle rather than to write a
// sharper shv2-specific code — so E3005 and these four strings are the contract.
//
// **TWO SENTENCES BECAUSE THE TWO POSITIONS HAVE DIFFERENT READERS**, and the rewrite each needs is
// shaped differently: a declaration site is told to declare an alias, a cast site is shown the cast
// that would work. **ONE PRODUCER EACH** because four bare literals is four places a rename half-lands.
//
// ⚠ **THE EXAMPLE RANGE IS PART OF THE CONTRACT AND DIFFERS PER PRIMITIVE** — `int(0 to 100)` against
// `float(0.0 to 1.0)`, `MyInt` against `MyFloat` — so it is derived from the spelling in one place
// rather than written into both sentences.
//
// ⚠ **THERE IS NO `byte` ARM, AND ITS ABSENCE IS DERIVED.** The oracle has a third case because it has
// a `TokenType.Byte`; shv2 has no `byte` keyword at all (`Parser.ByteTypeName`), so `byte` reaches every
// type position as a plain identifier and can never arrive here. A dead arm would be a second home for
// a fact — and one that reads as though the set were a choice rather than a consequence.
function barePrimitiveAliasNameFor(spelling String) returns String
	if spelling == "int" 'anInt'
		return "MyInt"
	end 'anInt'
	if spelling == "float" 'aFloat'
		return "MyFloat"
	end 'aFloat'

	panic("barePrimitiveAliasNameFor: '{spelling}' is not a ranged primitive — `keywordIsARangedPrimitive` admits only `int` and `float`, so a third spelling means that predicate and this table have drifted")
end 'barePrimitiveAliasNameFor'

function barePrimitiveExampleRangeFor(spelling String) returns String
	if spelling == "int" 'anInt'
		return "0 to 100"
	end 'anInt'
	if spelling == "float" 'aFloat'
		return "0.0 to 1.0"
	end 'aFloat'

	panic("barePrimitiveExampleRangeFor: '{spelling}' is not a ranged primitive — see `barePrimitiveAliasNameFor`")
end 'barePrimitiveExampleRangeFor'

export function barePrimitiveTypeMessage(spelling String) returns String
	return "Cannot use bare '{spelling}' as a type. Define a typealias with range constraints, e.g., typealias {barePrimitiveAliasNameFor(spelling)} = {spelling}({barePrimitiveExampleRangeFor(spelling)})"
end 'barePrimitiveTypeMessage'

export function barePrimitiveCastMessage(spelling String) returns String
	return "Cannot cast to bare '{spelling}'. Define a typealias with range constraints, e.g., 'value as {barePrimitiveAliasNameFor(spelling)}' where 'typealias {barePrimitiveAliasNameFor(spelling)} = {spelling}({barePrimitiveExampleRangeFor(spelling)})'"
end 'barePrimitiveCastMessage'

// `denotedNamedType` for the DECLARATION sites this pass walks — a parameter, a return type, a
// struct field. Each reports and recovers rather than failing the pass, so a file with three
// undeclared types reports three diagnostics.
//
// UNPOSITIONED, exactly like E3001/E3002 — nothing records a source span for a type REFERENCE in a
// DECLARATION (only ops and parameter NAMES carry one), so there is no line to point at. Positioning
// it means threading that span from `parseTypeReference` through `FuncSignature` into the registry
// this pass reads, which is its own piece of work. ⚠ It is NOT a property of the diagnostic: a cast
// target reaches the same verdict POSITIONED, because the parser holds the `as` token when it asks.
function resolveNamedType(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, id TypeNameId, readerFilePath FilePath) returns MaxonType
	let name = try project.typeNames.nameOf(id) otherwise panic("resolveTypes: named type id {id} is not in the project interner (merge did not fold this file's interner)")

	return try denotedNamedType(signatures, name: name, id: id, readerFilePath: readerFilePath) otherwise 'undeclared'
		reportDiagnostic(project, diagnostic: Diagnostic.create(ErrorCode.unknownType, message: unknownTypeMessage(String.from(name))))

		// A RECOVERY type, not a sentinel answer: `PassPipeline.run` gates on diagnostics
		// after EVERY pass and `resolveTypes` IS a pass, so the build throws the moment
		// this pass returns. Nothing downstream ever reads it — it exists only to keep
		// the module structurally well-formed for the instant in between, so that the
		// rest of this pass can go on to report the OTHER unknown types in the file
		// rather than stopping at the first.
		return MaxonType.integer
	end 'undeclared'
end 'resolveNamedType'
